/* Paper 1: 42 case clusters, 336 original questions from user-supplied study PDF. */
window.MED_PDF_PAPER1=[{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-01","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 19-year-old student develops widespread itchy wheals, lip swelling and a hoarse voice within 12 minutes of eating a dessert. She is wheezing and anxious. BP 122/76 mmHg, pulse 118/min, respiratory rate 26/min, oxygen saturation 93% on room air. She has previously had eczema and allergic rhinitis.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly establishes a life-threatening systemic reaction rather than uncomplicated urticaria?","options":["Past history of atopy","Widespread pruritic wheals","Acute hoarseness with wheeze","Sinus tachycardia"],"answer":"C","explain":"Acute hoarseness with wheeze. Airway and breathing involvement following a likely allergen indicate anaphylaxis, even with preserved blood pressure."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immunological sequence best explains a classic IgE-mediated reaction on re-exposure?","options":["Immune-complex deposition and complement consumption","Allergen cross-linking of mast-cell-bound IgE","Cytotoxic T-cell killing of epithelial cells","IgG blockade of a hormone receptor"],"answer":"B","explain":"Allergen cross-linking of mast-cell-bound IgE. Cross-linking of FcεRI-bound IgE triggers mast-cell degranulation (Type I hypersensitivity)."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which supervised intervention must take priority?","options":["Intramuscular adrenaline in the anterolateral thigh","Intravenous antihistamine to reduce the wheals","Nebulised salbutamol before any systemic drug","Blood sampling for serum tryptase"],"answer":"A","explain":"Intramuscular adrenaline in the anterolateral thigh. Intramuscular adrenaline treats airway oedema, bronchospasm and circulatory effects; investigation and adjuncts must not delay it."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which receptor effect of adrenaline most directly reduces mucosal oedema?","options":["β1-mediated cardiac stimulation","β2-mediated bronchodilation","M2 receptor antagonism","α1-mediated vasoconstriction"],"answer":"D","explain":"α1-mediated vasoconstriction. α1 stimulation contracts vascular smooth muscle and reduces airway mucosal swelling."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"After improvement, which statement best explains the need for further observation?","options":["The allergen is only cleared once the rash disappears","A second phase of symptoms may occur without new exposure","Tryptase remains elevated until the next day in every patient","Hypotension is inevitable after initial recovery"],"answer":"B","explain":"A second phase of symptoms may occur without new exposure. Biphasic anaphylaxis can cause recurrent symptoms after apparent resolution."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — After the first intervention the patient becomes more breathless: SpO₂ falls from 93% to 87%, wheeze becomes barely audible and she cannot speak full sentences. How should this change be interpreted?","options":["Reduced wheeze confirms bronchodilation","Anxiety is now the leading cause of dyspnoea","An impending respiratory emergency requires immediate escalation","This excludes anaphylaxis because the rash is improving"],"answer":"C","explain":"An impending respiratory emergency requires immediate escalation. A silent or near-silent chest with falling oxygen saturation indicates critical airflow obstruction, not recovery."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the Gell–Coombs hypersensitivity type in this classic mechanism.","answer":"Type I","explain":"Type I. IgE-dependent mast-cell degranulation is an immediate Type I hypersensitivity reaction."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the mast-cell enzyme measured after the reaction to support the diagnosis.","answer":"Tryptase","explain":"Tryptase. Serum mast-cell tryptase can support anaphylaxis retrospectively; it must never delay treatment."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-02","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 48-year-old man develops flushing, throat tightness and wheeze immediately after receiving iodinated contrast. He has never received this contrast before. He is pale and frightened, without visible urticaria. His pulse is 126/min and BP is 96/58 mmHg.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which conclusion is most appropriate given the absence of visible urticaria?","options":["A systemic reaction is unlikely without a rash","A non-IgE mechanism excludes anaphylaxis treatment","The episode is necessarily a vasovagal reaction","Anaphylaxis remains possible and requires urgent treatment"],"answer":"D","explain":"Anaphylaxis remains possible and requires urgent treatment. Severe systemic reactions may have no skin manifestations, and non-IgE-mediated activation may appear clinically identical."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mediator effect best explains the combination of flushing and falling blood pressure?","options":["Widespread arteriolar dilation and plasma leakage","Isolated coronary artery spasm","Erythrocyte destruction by complement","Increased systemic vascular resistance"],"answer":"A","explain":"Widespread arteriolar dilation and plasma leakage. Vasoactive mediators cause reduced vascular tone and increased vascular permeability."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"A competing diagnosis is vasovagal syncope. Which finding would favour it over anaphylaxis?","options":["Acute stridor after exposure","Bradycardia with rapid improvement on lying flat","Generalised bronchospasm","Progressive tongue swelling"],"answer":"B","explain":"Bradycardia with rapid improvement on lying flat. Vasovagal syncope often features bradycardia and positional improvement rather than respiratory or mucosal compromise."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which investigation would be most useful later to identify a specific IgE-mediated trigger when appropriate?","options":["A single total serum IgE level at presentation","Routine CT pulmonary angiography","Allergen-specific testing at specialist follow-up","A D-dimer during the acute event"],"answer":"C","explain":"Allergen-specific testing at specialist follow-up. Post-event allergy assessment may include directed skin-prick or specific IgE testing, guided by the suspected trigger."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which discharge communication would most improve safety after stabilisation?","options":["Avoid documenting the contrast until confirmatory testing","Give an allergy/reaction record and clear future exposure plan","Advise that future contrast is always safe if diluted","Replace emergency follow-up with oral antihistamines"],"answer":"B","explain":"Give an allergy/reaction record and clear future exposure plan. Documentation, trigger evaluation, and patient education reduce risk during future medical procedures."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — A clinician obtains serum tryptase after stabilisation. Acute level: 19 µg/L; later baseline: 4 µg/L. Which interpretation is best?","options":["An acute rise supports mast-cell activation in the clinical context","A normal baseline proves the event was not anaphylaxis","Tryptase identifies iodinated contrast as the specific trigger","The values exclude non-IgE mast-cell activation"],"answer":"A","explain":"An acute rise supports mast-cell activation in the clinical context. A marked acute increase from baseline supports mast-cell activation but does not identify the trigger or prove an IgE mechanism."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the immediate effector cell involved in histamine release.","answer":"Mast cell","explain":"Mast cell. Mast-cell activation can occur through IgE-dependent and non-IgE pathways."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the term for recurrence after initial recovery without further exposure.","answer":"Biphasic reaction","explain":"Biphasic reaction. A biphasic reaction may occur after symptoms have initially resolved."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-03","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 37-year-old woman receives an intravenous antibiotic. Within minutes she develops swollen lips, diffuse wheals, wheeze, confusion and a BP of 70/38 mmHg. Her skin is initially warm and flushed; the pulse is 142/min. Staff immediately stop the infusion and call for emergency assistance.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which shock mechanism is most likely dominant?","options":["Failure of the left ventricle to generate pressure","Mechanical obstruction of venous return","Reduced vascular resistance with capillary leakage","Whole-blood loss from a hidden haemorrhage"],"answer":"C","explain":"Reduced vascular resistance with capillary leakage. Anaphylactic shock is primarily distributive, with profound vasodilation and effective intravascular volume loss."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"How does increased vascular permeability worsen hypotension?","options":["Increased plasma protein synthesis raises oncotic pressure","Interstitial plasma loss reduces venous return and preload","Activation of the renin–angiotensin system immediately stops cardiac output","An increase in blood viscosity prevents capillary flow"],"answer":"B","explain":"Interstitial plasma loss reduces venous return and preload. Plasma extravasation lowers circulating volume, venous return, stroke volume and cardiac output."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pair of interventions best addresses the two principal circulatory problems?","options":["Intramuscular adrenaline and intravenous crystalloid","Intravenous antihistamine and oral rehydration","Nebulised salbutamol and intravenous steroid alone","Intravenous furosemide and a beta blocker"],"answer":"A","explain":"Intramuscular adrenaline and intravenous crystalloid. Adrenaline restores vascular tone and supports airway/breathing; crystalloid addresses intravascular fluid loss."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Why are warm extremities consistent with this type of shock early on?","options":["Severe bradycardia increases cutaneous oxygen delivery","Peripheral arterioles are extensively dilated","Sympathetic outflow has ceased completely","The skin becomes warm because oxygen content has normalised"],"answer":"B","explain":"Peripheral arterioles are extensively dilated. Reduced systemic vascular resistance can produce warm, flushed extremities in early distributive shock."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which condition should most urgently prompt escalation beyond routine supervised care?","options":["Transient itching after the rash resolves","Stable BP with improving wheeze","Mild discomfort at the injection site","Persistent hypotension despite initial emergency treatment"],"answer":"D","explain":"Persistent hypotension despite initial emergency treatment. Continued circulatory compromise risks organ hypoperfusion and requires expert-led critical care."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — After initial emergency measures: BP 74/42 mmHg, pulse 136/min, lactate 5.1 mmol/L (reference <2.0). Which interpretation is most defensible?","options":["Lactate confirms cardiogenic rather than distributive shock","The lactate is expected and rules out danger","Persistent hypoperfusion is likely despite warm skin","The lactate proves a primary seizure caused the collapse"],"answer":"C","explain":"Persistent hypoperfusion is likely despite warm skin. High lactate with persistent severe hypotension signals inadequate tissue perfusion; warm skin does not exclude shock."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the haemodynamic variable that falls markedly in distributive shock (abbreviation accepted).","answer":"SVR","explain":"SVR. Systemic vascular resistance falls through mediator-induced vasodilation."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Which adrenergic receptor mediates the key vasoconstrictor action of adrenaline?","answer":"Alpha-1","explain":"Alpha-1. α1 receptor activation counteracts vasodilation and mucosal oedema."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-04","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 63-year-old man taking propranolol for a cardiac condition is stung by a wasp. He rapidly becomes dyspnoeic, develops facial swelling and collapses. Emergency staff treat suspected anaphylactic shock. He remains hypotensive despite two appropriate intramuscular adrenaline doses and fluid resuscitation.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which chronic medication may contribute to a reduced response to adrenaline?","options":["Propranolol","Amlodipine","Omeprazole","Metformin"],"answer":"A","explain":"Propranolol. Non-selective beta blockade can blunt the β-adrenergic effects of adrenaline."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which additional medication may be considered by the emergency team because of this interaction?","options":["Diphenhydramine as a replacement for adrenaline","Furosemide to mobilise fluid into vessels","Glucagon to increase intracellular cAMP independently of β receptors","Vitamin K to restore vascular tone"],"answer":"C","explain":"Glucagon to increase intracellular cAMP independently of β receptors. Glucagon activates its own receptor to raise cAMP despite β-receptor blockade in refractory anaphylaxis."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the most appropriate escalation if shock persists after two intramuscular adrenaline doses?","options":["Observe without monitoring because skin signs are fading","Supervised intravenous adrenaline infusion in a monitored setting","Replace adrenaline with oral antihistamine","Delay circulation management until skin testing"],"answer":"B","explain":"Supervised intravenous adrenaline infusion in a monitored setting. Refractory anaphylactic shock needs specialist-led monitored vasoactive support and ongoing resuscitation."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which management error carries a major safety risk?","options":["Keeping the patient lying flat when tolerated","Using oxygen and continuous monitoring","Stopping the suspected trigger","Giving concentrated intramuscular-strength adrenaline as an unmonitored IV bolus"],"answer":"D","explain":"Giving concentrated intramuscular-strength adrenaline as an unmonitored IV bolus. Unmonitored IV boluses of concentrated adrenaline can cause catastrophic arrhythmias and hypertension."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"The same clinical syndrome occurs during a blood transfusion. Which immediate systems action is essential?","options":["Continue the unit at a slower rate","Discard the blood bag before an investigation","Stop transfusion and notify the blood bank","Wait until fever develops before reporting"],"answer":"C","explain":"Stop transfusion and notify the blood bank. A suspected severe transfusion reaction requires stopping transfusion, preserving relevant materials and blood-bank notification."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — On monitor: BP 68/36, sinus rate 132/min, SpO₂ 89% with persistent wheeze. Which interpretation best guides the student’s next role?","options":["Severe refractory airway and circulatory failure: call senior resuscitation support","Stable compensated shock suitable for clinic review","An uncomplicated cutaneous allergy despite hypotension","A drug rash unrelated to airway compromise"],"answer":"A","explain":"Severe refractory airway and circulatory failure: call senior resuscitation support. Persistent hypotension and hypoxaemia despite initial treatment demand urgent critical-care escalation, not independent student management."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the signalling messenger raised by glucagon despite β blockade.","answer":"cAMP","explain":"cAMP. Glucagon activates adenylate cyclase and raises cyclic AMP independent of β-adrenoceptors."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the safety-monitoring system for reporting transfusion adverse reactions.","answer":"Haemovigilance","explain":"Haemovigilance. Haemovigilance tracks and investigates transfusion-related adverse events."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-05","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 23-year-old woman reports photosensitive facial rash, painless oral ulcers, fatigue and intermittent non-erosive pain in both wrists. Her BP is 154/94 mmHg. Urine dipstick shows 3+ protein and 2+ blood. Examination reveals a malar eruption sparing the nasolabial folds.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pathogenic mechanism best explains simultaneous skin, joint and glomerular injury?","options":["IgE-driven immediate mast-cell degranulation","Deposition of circulating immune complexes with complement activation","Isolated bacterial invasion of synovial tissue","Direct T-cell destruction of pancreatic β cells"],"answer":"B","explain":"Deposition of circulating immune complexes with complement activation. SLE typically produces Type III immune-complex injury in skin, joints, serosa and kidneys."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which antibody most usefully helps assess disease activity and possible renal involvement?","options":["Anti-centromere","Anti-mitochondrial","Anti-histone","Anti-double-stranded DNA"],"answer":"D","explain":"Anti-double-stranded DNA. Anti-dsDNA is relatively specific and often correlates with SLE activity, particularly nephritis."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which additional finding best supports active immune-complex consumption?","options":["Low serum C3 and C4","Isolated elevated IgE","Raised troponin without symptoms","Low serum potassium"],"answer":"A","explain":"Low serum C3 and C4. Classical complement activation by immune complexes consumes C3 and C4."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which renal finding is most concerning for active glomerular inflammation?","options":["Trace ketones after an overnight fast","Isolated bacteriuria without urinary symptoms","Red-cell casts with proteinuria","Low urine specific gravity after drinking water"],"answer":"C","explain":"Red-cell casts with proteinuria. Red-cell casts are strong evidence of a renal glomerular source of bleeding."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which course of action is safest for a student confronted with these findings?","options":["Reassure because lupus arthritis is usually non-erosive","Urgently discuss suspected lupus nephritis and renal work-up with the supervising team","Begin nephrotoxic analgesics as sole therapy","Delay evaluation until the skin eruption has resolved"],"answer":"B","explain":"Urgently discuss suspected lupus nephritis and renal work-up with the supervising team. Proteinuria, haematuria and hypertension raise concern for organ-threatening lupus nephritis."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Investigations: ANA 1:640 positive; anti-dsDNA raised; C3 0.42 g/L (low); creatinine rises from 62 to 126 µmol/L in one month. Which interpretation is best?","options":["Positive ANA alone proves harmless cutaneous lupus","Rising creatinine excludes autoimmune glomerular disease","Normal urine findings are expected in severe nephritis","Results support active systemic disease with possible renal injury"],"answer":"D","explain":"Results support active systemic disease with possible renal injury. Rising creatinine plus immune markers and abnormal urine indicate potentially active organ-threatening disease."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the Gell–Coombs mechanism responsible for immune-complex nephritis.","answer":"Type III","explain":"Type III. SLE nephritis is a classic Type III hypersensitivity manifestation."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the classic glomerular capillary-wall appearance in diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis.","answer":"Wire-loop lesions","explain":"Wire-loop lesions. Large subendothelial immune deposits can produce wire-loop appearances."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-06","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 29-year-old woman with established SLE presents with a swollen painful calf. She has had two previous pregnancy losses. Her activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) is prolonged; platelet count is mildly reduced. A prior panel detected lupus anticoagulant.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which syndrome most coherently explains the combination of findings?","options":["Isolated factor VIII deficiency","Disseminated intravascular coagulation from infection","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Hereditary von Willebrand disease"],"answer":"C","explain":"Antiphospholipid syndrome. Antiphospholipid syndrome causes thrombosis and pregnancy morbidity, often with prolonged aPTT in laboratory assays."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Why can a prolonged aPTT coexist with a clinical tendency to thrombosis?","options":["The autoantibody interferes with phospholipid-dependent tests in vitro but is prothrombotic in vivo","The result always reflects severe depletion of all coagulation factors","Platelets stop aggregating when lupus anticoagulant is present","aPTT only measures bleeding risk and never indicates pathology"],"answer":"A","explain":"The autoantibody interferes with phospholipid-dependent tests in vitro but is prothrombotic in vivo. Lupus anticoagulant prolongs phospholipid-dependent clotting assays, yet promotes thrombotic events in patients."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which antibody is associated with neonatal lupus and congenital heart block?","options":["Anti-Smith","Anti-histone","Anti-Jo-1","Anti-Ro/SSA"],"answer":"D","explain":"Anti-Ro/SSA. Maternal anti-Ro/SSA (with or without anti-La) can cross the placenta and cause neonatal lupus/heart block."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which long-term medication commonly reduces SLE flares and warrants retinal monitoring?","options":["Amoxicillin","Hydroxychloroquine","Propranolol","Fluconazole"],"answer":"B","explain":"Hydroxychloroquine. Hydroxychloroquine is foundational SLE therapy; retinal toxicity is an important monitoring issue."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"She develops fever while taking high-dose immunosuppression. Which clinical approach is safest?","options":["Attribute fever to lupus without further assessment","Increase immunosuppression immediately before examination","Urgently evaluate for infection while assessing a possible flare","Ignore fever if anti-dsDNA has previously been positive"],"answer":"C","explain":"Urgently evaluate for infection while assessing a possible flare. Infection can rapidly become life-threatening in immunosuppressed patients and must be actively considered."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — A different patient takes hydralazine for several months and develops fever, arthralgia and serositis. Anti-histone is positive, anti-dsDNA negative and urine remains normal. What does this profile favour?","options":["Drug-induced lupus","Diffuse proliferative lupus nephritis","Antiphospholipid syndrome","IgE-mediated drug allergy"],"answer":"A","explain":"Drug-induced lupus. Anti-histone positivity with a typical medication exposure and relative renal sparing supports drug-induced lupus."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Which class of hypersensitivity also explains autoimmune haemolysis in SLE?","answer":"Type II","explain":"Type II. Cell-directed antibodies can produce Type II cytotoxic injury, distinct from immune-complex nephritis."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the sterile valvular vegetations associated with SLE.","answer":"Libman–Sacks","explain":"Libman–Sacks. Libman–Sacks endocarditis causes sterile vegetations on cardiac valves."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-07","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 31-year-old man reports weight loss, persistent oral thrush and recurrent respiratory infections. He consents to HIV testing, which is confirmed positive according to the clinic algorithm. His CD4 count is 96 cells/µL and HIV viral load is 165,000 copies/mL.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which HIV envelope molecule binds CD4 during initial attachment?","options":["p24","gp120","Integrase","Reverse transcriptase"],"answer":"B","explain":"gp120. The external HIV glycoprotein gp120 binds CD4 and a co-receptor such as CCR5."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the central immunological consequence of progressive CD4 T-cell depletion?","options":["Selective reduction in platelet production only","Isolated loss of red-cell membrane proteins","Loss of neutrophil respiratory burst","Impaired cell-mediated control of opportunistic pathogens"],"answer":"D","explain":"Impaired cell-mediated control of opportunistic pathogens. CD4 helper-cell loss undermines Th1 and macrophage responses and susceptibility to opportunistic infection rises."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which assay best measures response to antiretroviral treatment over time?","options":["Serial HIV RNA viral load","Total serum IgE","Coagulation international normalised ratio","Serum C3 alone"],"answer":"A","explain":"Serial HIV RNA viral load. HIV RNA viral load is the preferred virological measure of ART response."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"In a 6-week-old infant born to a mother with HIV, which diagnostic test is most appropriate?","options":["Maternal HIV antibody titre","Routine CD4 count as sole diagnostic test","HIV nucleic-acid testing (PCR)","Monospot heterophile antibody test"],"answer":"C","explain":"HIV nucleic-acid testing (PCR). Maternal anti-HIV IgG can cross the placenta, making infant antibody tests unreliable; viral nucleic-acid detection is used."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which drug–target pair is correct?","options":["Dolutegravir — protease","Lamivudine — viral integrase","Tenofovir — gp120 receptor","Dolutegravir — viral integrase"],"answer":"D","explain":"Dolutegravir — viral integrase. Dolutegravir inhibits HIV integrase, preventing viral DNA integration into host DNA."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — After starting ART, viral load falls to 45 copies/mL and CD4 rises to 242 cells/µL. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","options":["Viral replication remains as high as before therapy","There is a strong virological response with improving immune status","The result proves permanent viral eradication","A rising CD4 level excludes every opportunistic infection"],"answer":"B","explain":"There is a strong virological response with improving immune status. A viral load below 50 copies/mL indicates marked suppression in this study guide; CD4 recovery shows improved immune status, not cure."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the viral enzyme that converts RNA into DNA.","answer":"Reverse transcriptase","explain":"Reverse transcriptase. Reverse transcriptase produces a DNA copy of the HIV RNA genome."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the structural HIV capsid antigen commonly used in fourth-generation assays.","answer":"p24","explain":"p24. Fourth-generation screening detects p24 antigen as well as anti-HIV antibodies."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-08","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 36-year-old woman on TLD (tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir) starts rifampicin-based treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis. Four weeks after ART initiation, fever recurs and her lymph nodes enlarge despite a rapidly falling viral load. A nurse also reports a needlestick injury.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which drug interaction is most important when prescribing the TLD regimen with rifampicin?","options":["Rifampicin inhibits HIV protease directly","Rifampicin induces drug-metabolising pathways, reducing dolutegravir exposure","Rifampicin increases lamivudine absorption until it becomes toxic","Rifampicin permanently inhibits tenofovir excretion"],"answer":"B","explain":"Rifampicin induces drug-metabolising pathways, reducing dolutegravir exposure. Rifampicin induces relevant CYP/UGT pathways and lowers dolutegravir exposure, requiring regimen review by the treating team."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which explanation best fits worsening inflammatory signs while viral load falls after ART initiation?","options":["Immediate loss of viral susceptibility to every ART drug","Permanent irreversible CD4 suppression","Drug-induced IgE anaphylaxis","Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome"],"answer":"D","explain":"Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome. IRIS is an excessive inflammatory response to existing pathogen antigens as immune function recovers."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which action is most appropriate immediately after a significant occupational needlestick?","options":["Urgent exposure assessment and indicated HIV PEP","Wait for seroconversion before seeking care","Provide TB prevention in place of HIV PEP","Assume clean-looking needles cannot transmit HIV"],"answer":"A","explain":"Urgent exposure assessment and indicated HIV PEP. Time-sensitive exposure assessment and indicated PEP should not await a later antibody test."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"A patient fears disclosure of their HIV status to relatives. Which approach best upholds ethical practice?","options":["Inform family members automatically as a standard clinic policy","Refuse all care until the family is informed","Respect confidentiality and support voluntary disclosure","Record the status in a publicly visible register"],"answer":"C","explain":"Respect confidentiality and support voluntary disclosure. Respecting confidentiality, counselling and informed consent are key patient-centred HIV care principles."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which additional public-health action is most appropriate for someone newly diagnosed with pulmonary TB?","options":["Avoid healthcare visits until symptoms stop","Trace and screen close contacts","Treat household contacts with ART without testing","Assume an initial negative smear excludes transmission"],"answer":"B","explain":"Trace and screen close contacts. Contact evaluation and screening are core TB infection-control and public-health measures."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Viral load falls from 210,000 to 290 copies/mL and CD4 rises from 48 to 105 cells/µL in six weeks, but TB symptoms worsen. Which conclusion is strongest?","options":["A falling viral load rules out any inflammatory complication","The CD4 count proves the TB treatment has failed","The patient must discontinue all ART without assessment","Virological improvement makes IRIS plausible but other causes still require evaluation"],"answer":"D","explain":"Virological improvement makes IRIS plausible but other causes still require evaluation. Worsening after ART with virological response suggests IRIS; infection progression, resistance and other diagnoses must still be assessed."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the syndrome of paradoxical inflammatory worsening after immune recovery.","answer":"IRIS","explain":"IRIS. Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome may occur after ART is started."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the prevention strategy used immediately after a qualifying high-risk HIV exposure.","answer":"PEP","explain":"PEP. Post-exposure prophylaxis is time-sensitive following qualifying occupational or sexual exposures."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-09","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 38-year-old man with previously untreated HIV presents with painless violaceous plaques on both lower legs and a purple nodule on his hard palate. The plaques do not blanch. He has progressive bilateral leg oedema. Skin biopsy shows spindle-shaped cells, extravasated erythrocytes and irregular slit-like vascular spaces.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which underlying infectious agent is most strongly associated with this tumour?","options":["Epstein–Barr virus","Human papillomavirus 16","Human herpesvirus 8","Cytomegalovirus"],"answer":"C","explain":"Human herpesvirus 8. Kaposi sarcoma is driven by Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus, also called HHV-8."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which histological pattern is most supportive of this diagnosis?","options":["Keratin pearls with invasive squamous nests","Spindle cells surrounding slit-like vascular spaces","Well-formed caseating granulomas","Large atypical lymphocytes forming sheets"],"answer":"B","explain":"Spindle cells surrounding slit-like vascular spaces. KS characteristically shows spindle cells, slit-like vessels, haemorrhage and haemosiderin."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which viral protein is commonly demonstrated by immunohistochemistry in biopsy tissue?","options":["HIV reverse transcriptase","HPV E6","EBV EBNA2","HHV-8 LANA"],"answer":"D","explain":"HHV-8 LANA. Latency-associated nuclear antigen (LANA) supports HHV-8-associated Kaposi sarcoma."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which process helps explain development of the lesions?","options":["Viral oncogenic signalling promotes spindle-cell proliferation and angiogenesis","IgE cross-linking causes transient wheals without tumour growth","A thrombus blocks blood supply to every lesion","Systemic complement deficiency directly transforms keratinocytes"],"answer":"A","explain":"Viral oncogenic signalling promotes spindle-cell proliferation and angiogenesis. HHV-8 drives oncogenic signalling and angiogenesis, leading to vascular proliferation."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which initial management principle is most appropriate?","options":["Apply topical nystatin and review the lesion only if painful","Give high-dose antihistamines as definitive cancer therapy","Assess HIV status and disease extent, optimise ART and refer appropriately","Avoid tissue diagnosis whenever the lesion is on the palate"],"answer":"C","explain":"Assess HIV status and disease extent, optimise ART and refer appropriately. KS requires biopsy-supported diagnosis, HIV assessment, ART and referral, especially for symptomatic or extensive disease."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Results: HIV confirmed; CD4 58 cells/µL; viral load 310,000 copies/mL; haemoglobin 10.0 g/dL. Purple palatal and skin lesions are seen. Which is the most defensible interpretation?","options":["A high CD4 count makes KS unlikely","Advanced immunosuppression raises concern for HIV-associated KS","The haemoglobin confirms purely benign vascular lesions","The viral load identifies HHV-8 directly"],"answer":"B","explain":"Advanced immunosuppression raises concern for HIV-associated KS. Advanced HIV and characteristic lesions strongly support HIV-associated KS, but biopsy confirms the tumour."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the oncogenic herpesvirus associated with Kaposi sarcoma.","answer":"HHV-8","explain":"HHV-8. HHV-8 is also called Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — What is the diagnostic tissue procedure for a suspicious accessible lesion?","answer":"Biopsy","explain":"Biopsy. Histological assessment with immunostaining can establish the diagnosis."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-10","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 42-year-old woman living with HIV has multiple confirmed cutaneous Kaposi sarcoma plaques. She now presents with progressive dyspnoea, haemoptysis and reduced exercise tolerance. Examination identifies purple oral lesions and dependent oedema; a chest radiograph demonstrates diffuse nodular/interstitial abnormalities.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which important complication should be considered?","options":["Isolated allergic rhinitis","Myocardial infarction confined to the right ventricle","A purely cosmetic progression with no systemic risk","Visceral Kaposi sarcoma involving the lungs"],"answer":"D","explain":"Visceral Kaposi sarcoma involving the lungs. Pulmonary KS may cause dyspnoea, haemoptysis, infiltrates and pleural abnormalities."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which differential is particularly relevant because of her immune status and radiograph?","options":["Pulmonary tuberculosis or Pneumocystis infection","Cytomegalovirus retinitis","Cryptococcal meningitis","Oesophageal candidiasis"],"answer":"A","explain":"Pulmonary tuberculosis or Pneumocystis infection. In advanced HIV, pulmonary infections and malignancy can have overlapping clinical or radiographic findings."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which additional symptom could indicate gastrointestinal KS involvement?","options":["New-onset isolated photophobia","Recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding","Isolated intermittent rhinitis","Newly painful cervical nodes"],"answer":"B","explain":"Recurrent gastrointestinal bleeding. Visceral KS can affect the gastrointestinal tract and cause occult or overt bleeding."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature would better fit bacillary angiomatosis as a differential for reddish-purple lesions?","options":["HHV-8 antigen detected on biopsy","EBV-positive lymphoid proliferation","Bartonella-associated proliferative vascular lesions","Dermal Candida pseudohyphae"],"answer":"C","explain":"Bartonella-associated proliferative vascular lesions. Bacillary angiomatosis is a Bartonella-associated vascular proliferation that can mimic cutaneous KS in HIV."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest next step for a GEMP1 student assessing this breathless patient?","options":["Wait for an outpatient HIV viral load before respiratory assessment","Assume oral candidiasis explains every respiratory symptom","Start treatment for tuberculosis without first assessing the hypoxaemia","Escalate for prompt respiratory evaluation and assessment of visceral disease"],"answer":"D","explain":"Escalate for prompt respiratory evaluation and assessment of visceral disease. Haemoptysis and dyspnoea in a patient with KS require prompt assessment for serious pulmonary disease."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Data: respiratory rate 30/min, SpO₂ 88% on room air, pulse 124/min, BP 108/68 mmHg. Which finding most directly establishes need for urgent respiratory support?","options":["SpO₂ 88% with marked tachypnoea","The presence of a previous skin biopsy","A normal-range systolic blood pressure","Chronic purple plaques without acute change"],"answer":"A","explain":"SpO₂ 88% with marked tachypnoea. Hypoxaemia with tachypnoea indicates significant respiratory compromise regardless of the underlying cause."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — What term describes tumour involvement of internal organs?","answer":"Visceral involvement","explain":"Visceral involvement. Pulmonary and gastrointestinal KS are forms of visceral disease."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the major immune-restoring treatment in HIV-associated KS.","answer":"Antiretroviral therapy","explain":"Antiretroviral therapy. ART is foundational in HIV-associated KS, sometimes supplemented by specialist cancer therapy."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-11","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 25-year-old student returns from visiting family in northern Mozambique. Seven days later he has fever, rigors, headache and myalgia. He is alert, drinks normally and has no respiratory distress. BP 118/74 mmHg, glucose 5.3 mmol/L, creatinine 84 µmol/L and platelet count 96 × 10⁹/L. A malaria rapid diagnostic test is positive.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which species most urgently needs consideration because of its severe-disease potential in southern Africa?","options":["Plasmodium malariae","Plasmodium ovale","Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium vivax"],"answer":"C","explain":"Plasmodium falciparum. P. falciparum predominates in much of southern Africa and can rapidly produce life-threatening disease."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which stage is introduced into humans during an infected mosquito bite?","options":["Sporozoites","Gametocytes","Mature blood-stage schizonts","Hypnozoites"],"answer":"A","explain":"Sporozoites. An infected female Anopheles mosquito inoculates sporozoites, which migrate to the liver."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What produces recurrent fever paroxysms during blood-stage infection?","options":["Release of thyroid hormone","Rupture of infected erythrocytes with inflammatory mediator release","Immediate immune-complex deposition in joints","Mast-cell degranulation after each mosquito bite"],"answer":"B","explain":"Rupture of infected erythrocytes with inflammatory mediator release. Synchronous or asynchronous erythrocyte rupture releases parasites and stimulates inflammatory cytokine responses."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which blood test is best suited to quantify parasitaemia and help identify species?","options":["Single serum C-reactive protein","Urine dipstick microscopy","Serum tryptase","Thin blood film"],"answer":"D","explain":"Thin blood film. The thin smear preserves red-cell morphology and permits parasite identification and approximate parasite counts."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"If malaria is uncomplicated and confirmed, which treatment is a standard first-line option in the study materials?","options":["Artemether–lumefantrine","Intravenous artesunate for every stable patient","Primaquine as sole blood-stage therapy","Quinine monotherapy despite available ACT"],"answer":"A","explain":"Artemether–lumefantrine. Artemether–lumefantrine is an artemisinin-based combination therapy used for uncomplicated falciparum malaria."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Laboratory results: Hb 12.5 g/dL, platelets 96 × 10⁹/L, glucose 5.3 mmol/L, creatinine 84 µmol/L; patient alert, stable and tolerating oral intake. Which conclusion is best?","options":["Thrombocytopenia alone proves cerebral malaria","The data are compatible with malaria without the listed severe features, but close assessment remains necessary","A positive rapid test means blood films have no diagnostic value","Normal creatinine rules out malaria infection"],"answer":"B","explain":"The data are compatible with malaria without the listed severe features, but close assessment remains necessary. Low platelets are common in malaria; the listed data do not themselves show impaired consciousness, hypoglycaemia or organ failure."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the mosquito genus that transmits human malaria.","answer":"Anopheles","explain":"Anopheles. Female Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium parasites."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the antimalarial combination used for uncomplicated falciparum disease.","answer":"Artemether–lumefantrine","explain":"Artemether–lumefantrine. This artemisinin combination is used for uncomplicated falciparum malaria in the source notes."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-12","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 20-year-old man returns from a malaria-endemic region. He has fever, vomiting, dark urine and worsening confusion. BP 92/56 mmHg, pulse 128/min, glucose 2.2 mmol/L, creatinine 210 µmol/L, haemoglobin 7.0 g/dL, platelets 38 × 10⁹/L; malaria rapid test is positive and a blood film shows numerous P. falciparum ring forms.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pathophysiological mechanism best explains cerebral involvement in falciparum malaria?","options":["Long-lived hepatic hypnozoites entering the brain","Cytotoxic antibodies destroying neuronal myelin","Infected erythrocytes sequestering in cerebral microvessels","Direct sporozoite multiplication inside neurons"],"answer":"C","explain":"Infected erythrocytes sequestering in cerebral microvessels. PfEMP1-mediated endothelial adhesion and sequestration obstruct the microcirculation and contribute to cerebral disease."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immediate supervised approach is most appropriate?","options":["Oral primaquine alone and discharge","Urgent resuscitation, correction of hypoglycaemia, IV artesunate and admission","Wait for a second rapid test before addressing low glucose","Provide only oral antipyretics until creatinine normalises"],"answer":"B","explain":"Urgent resuscitation, correction of hypoglycaemia, IV artesunate and admission. Altered consciousness, hypoglycaemia and renal impairment indicate severe malaria requiring emergency care and parenteral treatment."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What causes the anaemia and dark urine in severe haemolytic malaria?","options":["Intravascular red-cell destruction with haemoglobin release","Selective platelet destruction without erythrocyte injury","Reduced iron absorption after one day of fever","A pure coagulation-factor deficiency"],"answer":"A","explain":"Intravascular red-cell destruction with haemoglobin release. Red-cell haemolysis can produce haemoglobinaemia, haemoglobinuria and anaemia."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which parasite surface-associated factor contributes to erythrocyte cytoadherence?","options":["HIV gp120","HHV-8 LANA","Candida germ tubes","PfEMP1"],"answer":"D","explain":"PfEMP1. PfEMP1 on infected red cells mediates adherence to vascular endothelium and sequestration."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"A separate patient has relapsing P. vivax malaria. Which safety check is needed before radical cure with primaquine?","options":["Serum anti-dsDNA titre","HLA-B*5701 typing","G6PD status","Serum mast-cell tryptase"],"answer":"C","explain":"G6PD status. Primaquine can cause haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, so testing is required before use."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Repeat assessment: glucose still 2.2 mmol/L, mental status worsening, creatinine 210 µmol/L. Which interpretation is safest?","options":["Severe malaria is present with immediate neurological, metabolic and renal danger","This is a typical uncomplicated fever because the malaria test is positive","Thrombocytopenia alone explains all neurological symptoms","A normal serum sodium would exclude organ dysfunction"],"answer":"A","explain":"Severe malaria is present with immediate neurological, metabolic and renal danger. Hypoglycaemia, encephalopathy and kidney injury are major danger features that need rapid correction and escalation."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the severe falciparum complication involving altered consciousness.","answer":"Cerebral malaria","explain":"Cerebral malaria. Cerebral malaria results in part from sequestration and microvascular pathology."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the parenteral antimalarial preferred for severe malaria.","answer":"IV artesunate","explain":"IV artesunate. Intravenous artesunate is preferred in severe falciparum malaria."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-13","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 46-year-old woman with asthma has recently increased her inhaled corticosteroid use and does not rinse her mouth afterward. She develops a sore mouth and curd-like white plaques on the buccal mucosa. Gentle scraping removes the plaques, revealing erythematous mucosa.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which organism most commonly causes this presentation?","options":["Cryptococcus neoformans","Candida albicans","Aspergillus fumigatus","Histoplasma capsulatum"],"answer":"B","explain":"Candida albicans. C. albicans is a common cause of pseudomembranous oral candidiasis."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism explains her predisposition?","options":["A rise in local mucosal cell-mediated defence","Antibody destruction of platelets","Local immune suppression from deposited inhaled corticosteroid","Increased production of gastric acid"],"answer":"C","explain":"Local immune suppression from deposited inhaled corticosteroid. Inhaled corticosteroid deposition suppresses mucosal antifungal defence, especially without mouth rinsing."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which microscopic finding would support the diagnosis?","options":["Budding yeasts and pseudohyphae","Acid-fast bacilli in cords","Intracellular Plasmodium rings","Spindle cells forming vascular slits"],"answer":"A","explain":"Budding yeasts and pseudohyphae. Candida may show budding yeast forms and pseudohyphae on scraping or microscopy."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment mechanism is associated with nystatin?","options":["Inhibits fungal lanosterol 14α-demethylase","Inhibits β-1,3-glucan synthesis in fungal cell walls","Inhibits fungal squalene epoxidase","Binds fungal ergosterol and disrupts membranes"],"answer":"D","explain":"Binds fungal ergosterol and disrupts membranes. Nystatin is a polyene that binds fungal membrane ergosterol and produces membrane leakage."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which preventive advice is most appropriate?","options":["Stop asthma treatment permanently without review","Use a spacer where appropriate and rinse the mouth after inhaled corticosteroids","Take systemic antibiotics after every inhaler dose","Avoid drinking water for several hours after using an inhaler"],"answer":"B","explain":"Use a spacer where appropriate and rinse the mouth after inhaled corticosteroids. Spacer use and oral rinsing reduce corticosteroid deposition in the mouth."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — On microscopy, the oral scraping demonstrates budding yeast and pseudohyphae. The patient is otherwise clinically stable with no painful swallowing. Which conclusion best matches these data?","options":["Invasive cryptococcal meningitis requiring lumbar puncture","HIV infection is definitively excluded","Localized oral candidiasis is supported; evaluate risk factors and treat appropriately","The microscopy confirms a premalignant leukoplakia"],"answer":"C","explain":"Localized oral candidiasis is supported; evaluate risk factors and treat appropriately. The clinical appearance and fungal forms support oral candidiasis; other risk factors may be assessed as indicated."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the main fungal sterol targeted by polyenes.","answer":"Ergosterol","explain":"Ergosterol. Polyenes such as nystatin bind ergosterol in fungal membranes."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the characteristic filamentous Candida structures on microscopy.","answer":"Pseudohyphae","explain":"Pseudohyphae. Pseudohyphae are elongated budding-cell chains often seen in Candida infection."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-bcmh-14","paper":"Paper 1","module":"BCMH","subject":"BCMH","scenario":"A 34-year-old man with poorly controlled HIV has weight loss, painful swallowing and several cream-white plaques on his tongue that scrape off. His CD4 count is 74 cells/µL. A separate corrugated white lesion on the lateral tongue does not scrape off.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which diagnosis most plausibly explains the new painful swallowing?","options":["Uncomplicated allergic rhinitis","Oral hairy leukoplakia alone","Kaposi sarcoma limited to the leg","Oesophageal candidiasis"],"answer":"D","explain":"Oesophageal candidiasis. Thrush with odynophagia in advanced HIV strongly suggests oesophageal candidiasis and warrants appropriate assessment/treatment."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which organism is associated with the non-scrapable corrugated lateral tongue lesion?","options":["Epstein–Barr virus","Human herpesvirus 8","Plasmodium falciparum","Candida albicans only"],"answer":"A","explain":"Epstein–Barr virus. Oral hairy leukoplakia is associated with EBV and classically does not scrape off."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment is most appropriate for suspected oesophageal candidiasis rather than isolated mild oral thrush?","options":["Topical nystatin as the sole treatment","Systemic fluconazole","Intramuscular adrenaline","Artemether–lumefantrine"],"answer":"B","explain":"Systemic fluconazole. Oesophageal candidiasis requires systemic antifungal therapy; topical treatment alone is insufficient."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which enzyme is inhibited by fluconazole?","options":["Human cyclooxygenase 1","HIV protease","Fungal lanosterol 14α-demethylase","Plasmodium dihydrofolate reductase"],"answer":"C","explain":"Fungal lanosterol 14α-demethylase. Azoles inhibit fungal CYP51/lanosterol 14α-demethylase, reducing ergosterol synthesis."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"If odynophagia fails to improve with appropriate antifungal therapy, which next diagnostic consideration is important?","options":["Assume all cases are resistant Candida and never investigate","Exclude all viral disease solely from a negative bacterial culture","Diagnose uncomplicated reflux without further assessment","Evaluate alternative oesophageal causes such as CMV or HSV with specialist input"],"answer":"D","explain":"Evaluate alternative oesophageal causes such as CMV or HSV with specialist input. Persistent symptoms require reassessment and consideration of viral oesophagitis or other pathology."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Data: CD4 74 cells/µL, non-scrapable corrugated lateral tongue lesion, scrapable oral plaques and odynophagia. Which interpretation is most accurate?","options":["Possible coexisting oral hairy leukoplakia and oesophageal candidiasis","The two oral lesions must be caused by the same microorganism","Normal immune competence is established by the CD4 value","The lesions are definitive evidence of Kaposi sarcoma"],"answer":"A","explain":"Possible coexisting oral hairy leukoplakia and oesophageal candidiasis. EBV-linked oral hairy leukoplakia may coexist with Candida infection in advanced HIV; the different plaque characteristics discriminate them."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the oral lesion caused by EBV that does not scrape off.","answer":"Oral hairy leukoplakia","explain":"Oral hairy leukoplakia. Oral hairy leukoplakia typically affects the lateral tongue and differs from scrapable Candida plaques."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the systemic azole commonly used for oesophageal candidiasis.","answer":"Fluconazole","explain":"Fluconazole. Fluconazole inhibits fungal ergosterol synthesis and is used for oesophageal candidiasis."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-01","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 52-year-old man attends a clinic for a routine check. Blood pressure is 168/104 mmHg on repeat measurement; pulse 84/min. He feels well. He has obesity, a high-salt diet and a family history of hypertension. Urinalysis is normal and there are no acute neurological or cardiac symptoms.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which vascular structure makes the largest contribution to systemic vascular resistance?","options":["Arterioles","Great veins","Large elastic arteries","Postcapillary venules"],"answer":"A","explain":"Arterioles. Small-radius arterioles are the main resistance vessels and determine much of systemic blood pressure."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which relationship most directly explains why persistently elevated resistance increases arterial pressure?","options":["MAP is determined by capillary oncotic pressure alone","MAP depends partly on cardiac output multiplied by vascular resistance","MAP varies inversely with circulating blood volume","MAP depends only on venous oxygen saturation"],"answer":"B","explain":"MAP depends partly on cardiac output multiplied by vascular resistance. Mean arterial pressure is approximately cardiac output times systemic vascular resistance."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which long-term myocardial adaptation is most likely with sustained high afterload?","options":["Right ventricular dilatation from low pulmonary flow","Concentric left ventricular hypertrophy","Isolated right atrial hypertrophy","Left ventricular thinning without remodelling"],"answer":"B","explain":"Concentric left ventricular hypertrophy. Chronic pressure overload leads to concentric left ventricular hypertrophy."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which renal mechanism can help maintain the high pressure?","options":["Complete cessation of renin release in every patient","RAAS-mediated sodium retention and vasoconstriction","Increased atrial natriuretic peptide alone","Suppression of all sodium reabsorption"],"answer":"B","explain":"RAAS-mediated sodium retention and vasoconstriction. Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone signalling can increase vascular tone and sodium/water retention."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the most appropriate supervised outpatient approach for this asymptomatic patient?","options":["Diagnose hypertensive emergency from the number alone","Ignore the result because there are no symptoms","Confirm persistent hypertension and assess risk and organ damage","Immediately administer intravenous antihypertensives"],"answer":"C","explain":"Confirm persistent hypertension and assess risk and organ damage. Repeat validated BP assessment and evaluate cardiovascular risk and organ damage; emergency requires acute organ injury."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Creatinine is normal, urine shows no protein or blood, and examination shows no acute target-organ injury. BP remains 166/102 mmHg. Best interpretation?","options":["Sustained hypertension without demonstrated acute organ injury","Cardiogenic shock is developing","Severe hypoglycaemia explains the pressure","Hypertensive emergency is confirmed"],"answer":"A","explain":"Sustained hypertension without demonstrated acute organ injury. An elevated BP without acute target-organ dysfunction does not by itself establish a hypertensive emergency."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the main hormone that promotes renal sodium retention in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system.","answer":"Aldosterone","explain":"Aldosterone. Aldosterone acts on the distal nephron to increase sodium reabsorption."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the vessel-wall cell layer damaged in hypertension and atherosclerosis.","answer":"Endothelium","explain":"Endothelium. Endothelial injury alters vascular tone and promotes lipid entry into arteries."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-02","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 47-year-old woman presents with a severe headache, blurred vision and new confusion. BP is 228/132 mmHg. Fundoscopy reveals retinal haemorrhages and swollen optic discs. Serum creatinine has risen from her previously documented baseline.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What makes this episode a hypertensive emergency rather than uncomplicated severe hypertension?","options":["History of inadequate exercise","A single elevated diastolic reading","Pulse above 100/min","Presence of acute target-organ injury"],"answer":"D","explain":"Presence of acute target-organ injury. Acute neurological, retinal and renal injury in the setting of markedly elevated BP defines a hypertensive emergency."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which vascular pathology is classically associated with severe accelerated hypertension?","options":["Isolated venous valve fibrosis","Atheromatous emboli in every arteriole","Caseating granulomas","Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with onion-skin change"],"answer":"D","explain":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis with onion-skin change. Severe hypertension can cause concentric arteriolar wall thickening and fibrinoid injury."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding provides the clearest clue to malignant hypertensive retinopathy?","options":["Presbyopia","Dry-eye irritation","Small physiologic cup-to-disc ratio","Optic disc swelling (papilloedema)"],"answer":"D","explain":"Optic disc swelling (papilloedema). Papilloedema with retinal haemorrhages is a red flag for hypertensive emergency."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Why can an abrupt, excessive BP reduction be dangerous?","options":["It immediately causes hyperthyroidism","It may compromise perfusion in organs with shifted autoregulation","It increases venous valve competence","It makes troponin negative"],"answer":"B","explain":"It may compromise perfusion in organs with shifted autoregulation. Cerebral and other organs may be adapted to higher perfusion pressures; excessive rapid reduction can cause ischaemia."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest action for a GEMP1 student?","options":["Treat only the headache and send her home","Ask for urgent senior-led assessment, monitoring and controlled therapy","Give an unsupervised rapid oral bolus to normalize BP immediately","Arrange review in six months"],"answer":"B","explain":"Ask for urgent senior-led assessment, monitoring and controlled therapy. Acute organ dysfunction requires immediate monitored senior-directed management."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Creatinine has increased 82 → 168 µmol/L, urine protein is 2+, and the patient remains confused. Which interpretation fits the trend?","options":["The abnormality only reflects a laboratory reference issue","The renal changes exclude hypertension","Acute renal and cerebral involvement are likely","No target-organ injury is present"],"answer":"C","explain":"Acute renal and cerebral involvement are likely. Rising creatinine plus altered mental status indicates acute target-organ injury."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — What is the name for optic disc swelling seen on fundoscopy?","answer":"Papilloedema","explain":"Papilloedema. Optic disc oedema is an alarming funduscopic feature."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the small-vessel lesion characterised by concentric onion-skin thickening.","answer":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","explain":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis. Concentric proliferative narrowing is associated with severe hypertension."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-03","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 59-year-old smoker with type 2 diabetes describes several months of central chest pressure while climbing stairs that settled within five minutes of rest. Today similar discomfort started at rest and lasted 35 minutes, with sweating and nausea.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which change is most concerning for acute coronary syndrome?","options":["Pain relieved by rest over previous months","Prolonged new pain occurring at rest","Age greater than 50 years","Pressure located behind the sternum"],"answer":"B","explain":"Prolonged new pain occurring at rest. Rest pain that is prolonged and new/changed suggests unstable coronary ischaemia."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the usual underlying process in acute atherothrombotic coronary events?","options":["Plaque disruption with platelet-rich thrombosis","Sudden destruction of the sinoatrial node by a virus","Primary pulmonary venous occlusion","Isolated acute aortic wall calcification"],"answer":"A","explain":"Plaque disruption with platelet-rich thrombosis. Plaque rupture or erosion promotes thrombosis and abrupt reduction in coronary flow."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the key myocardial imbalance causing ischaemic pain?","options":["Oxygen delivery becomes insufficient for myocardial demand","Low serum albumin causing myocardial oedema","Excess glucose delivery to myocytes","Excess oxygen supply relative to demand"],"answer":"A","explain":"Oxygen delivery becomes insufficient for myocardial demand. Ischaemia arises when coronary oxygen supply fails to meet myocardial oxygen demand."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which initial investigation must not be delayed in a patient with ongoing suspected ACS?","options":["Elective coronary calcium score","Routine outpatient exercise test","Electrocardiogram (ECG)","Spirometry"],"answer":"C","explain":"Electrocardiogram (ECG). An immediate 12-lead ECG is essential for identifying STEMI and dangerous rhythms."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which drug class directly limits platelet activation in suspected atherothrombotic ACS when appropriate?","options":["Thyroid hormones","Antifungals","Antihistamines","Antiplatelet agents"],"answer":"D","explain":"Antiplatelet agents. Platelet inhibition counteracts arterial thrombus formation; use is guided by the supervising ACS protocol."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — ECG shows horizontal ST depression in V4-V6; serial high-sensitivity troponin rises from 18 to 160 ng/L. Which conclusion is best?","options":["Stable exertional angina only","A myocardial infarction without ST elevation is likely","The ECG excludes cardiac ischaemia","Troponin is specific for pulmonary infection"],"answer":"B","explain":"A myocardial infarction without ST elevation is likely. A dynamic troponin rise with ischaemic symptoms and changes supports NSTEMI."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the myocardial biomarker used to detect cardiac injury.","answer":"Troponin","explain":"Troponin. Cardiac troponin I/T indicates myocardial injury but requires clinical context."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the artery-wall lesion that predisposes to plaque rupture.","answer":"Atherosclerotic plaque","explain":"Atherosclerotic plaque. Lipid-rich inflamed plaques are the substrate for coronary thrombosis."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-04","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 64-year-old man presents two hours after severe crushing chest pain with diaphoresis. BP 94/62 mmHg, pulse 54/min. A 12-lead ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF. He becomes dizzy and has cool extremities.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which anatomical region is most directly indicated by ST elevation in II, III and aVF?","options":["Inferior myocardium","Septal wall only","High lateral wall","Anterior left ventricular wall"],"answer":"A","explain":"Inferior myocardium. Leads II, III and aVF look at the inferior myocardial wall."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which coronary artery commonly supplies this territory in a right-dominant circulation?","options":["Left main coronary artery","Right coronary artery","Left anterior descending artery","Left circumflex marginal branch only"],"answer":"B","explain":"Right coronary artery. In right-dominant anatomy the right coronary artery usually supplies the inferior wall and often AV node."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains this patient’s bradycardia?","options":["Inferior ischaemia may impair the sinoatrial or AV nodal blood supply","Increased thyroid hormone secretion","Primary loss of pulmonary surfactant","Obligate increase in sympathetic firing"],"answer":"A","explain":"Inferior ischaemia may impair the sinoatrial or AV nodal blood supply. Inferior MI, often from RCA occlusion, can cause SA/AV nodal dysfunction and bradyarrhythmia."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which cellular outcome results from prolonged severe myocardial ischaemia?","options":["Coagulative necrosis of cardiomyocytes","Complete restoration of ATP in all cells","Reversible fatty change only","Granulomatous repair without cell death"],"answer":"A","explain":"Coagulative necrosis of cardiomyocytes. Prolonged severe ischaemia causes irreversible myocardial injury with coagulative necrosis."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the most urgent supervised management priority?","options":["Urgently activate the STEMI reperfusion pathway","Treat as musculoskeletal pain unless fever occurs","Arrange an outpatient stress test next week","Give routine high-flow oxygen despite normal saturation regardless of context"],"answer":"A","explain":"Urgently activate the STEMI reperfusion pathway. STEMI requires urgent reperfusion assessment and senior emergency management."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — ECG has ST elevation II, III, aVF and reciprocal depression in I/aVL. What does the pattern reinforce?","options":["Normal early repolarisation in all patients","Left ventricular hypertrophy without ischaemia","Inferior STEMI requiring urgent reperfusion assessment","Diffuse pericarditis is proven"],"answer":"C","explain":"Inferior STEMI requiring urgent reperfusion assessment. An inferior territory ST-elevation pattern with reciprocal changes strongly supports an acute infarction."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the dangerous high-grade conduction disorder that may follow AV nodal ischaemia.","answer":"Complete heart block","explain":"Complete heart block. Inferior infarction can compromise AV conduction and produce complete block."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — What type of necrosis is typical of an infarcted myocardium?","answer":"Coagulative necrosis","explain":"Coagulative necrosis. Ischaemic infarction produces coagulative necrosis in solid organs other than the brain."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-05","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 68-year-old man with a previous myocardial infarction reports progressive exertional dyspnoea, orthopnoea and ankle swelling. Examination shows basal crackles, an S3 gallop and a raised jugular venous pressure. An echocardiogram estimates left-ventricular ejection fraction at 30%.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What physiological dysfunction is most consistent with the reduced ejection fraction?","options":["Purely obstructive lung disease","Isolated increased pulmonary surfactant","Normal ventricular emptying with no pump impairment","Predominantly impaired systolic contraction"],"answer":"D","explain":"Predominantly impaired systolic contraction. A low EF indicates reduced ventricular systolic function (HFrEF)."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Why does left-sided heart failure cause basal crackles?","options":["Alveoli are destroyed by alpha-1 antitrypsin","Pleural pressure becomes uniformly positive during rest","Bronchial smooth muscle develops eosinophilic spasm","Fluid moves from pulmonary capillaries into lung interstitium/alveoli"],"answer":"D","explain":"Fluid moves from pulmonary capillaries into lung interstitium/alveoli. Elevated left atrial and pulmonary venous pressure causes pulmonary congestion and oedema."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What explains the raised JVP and dependent pitting oedema?","options":["Increased systemic venous pressure","A fall in total body sodium","Decreased hydrostatic pressure in leg capillaries","Reduced venous return from excessive diuresis alone"],"answer":"A","explain":"Increased systemic venous pressure. Systemic venous congestion raises peripheral hydrostatic pressure and drives oedema."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Why can chronic RAAS activation become harmful in heart failure?","options":["It consistently lowers afterload and sodium","It has no effect on fluid balance","It removes ventricular wall stress","It increases vasoconstriction, fluid retention and remodelling"],"answer":"D","explain":"It increases vasoconstriction, fluid retention and remodelling. Chronic neurohormonal activation worsens afterload, congestion and adverse structural remodelling."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which drug class inhibits harmful angiotensin-II signalling in a stable appropriate patient?","options":["Antimalarial artemisinins","Short-acting inhaled bronchodilators","ACE inhibitors or ARBs","Antifungal azoles"],"answer":"C","explain":"ACE inhibitors or ARBs. ACE inhibitors/ARBs suppress the maladaptive RAAS pathway in suitable heart-failure patients."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Echo EF 30%, NT-proBNP is increased and lungs have bilateral crackles. Which classification best fits?","options":["Right heart failure only with normal LV","A primary restrictive pulmonary disorder","Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction"],"answer":"C","explain":"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. EF 30% with the syndrome of congestion supports HFrEF."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the extra heart sound associated with rapid filling of a volume-overloaded ventricle.","answer":"S3","explain":"S3. A third heart sound can occur with increased filling pressures and volume overload."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the hormone system abbreviated RAAS.","answer":"Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","explain":"Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. RAAS activation contributes to vasoconstriction and salt retention."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-06","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 74-year-old woman with years of hypertension wakes abruptly gasping for breath. BP 202/112 mmHg, pulse 116/min, SpO₂ 82% on room air. She is sweaty, cannot complete sentences and has bilateral fine crackles. Echo performed previously showed a thick-walled LV and ejection fraction 60%.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What presentation is most concerning?","options":["An isolated anxiety attack","Simple allergic rhinitis","Stable obstructive sleep apnoea only","Acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema"],"answer":"D","explain":"Acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. Severe hypoxaemic dyspnoea with crackles and very high BP suggests acute pulmonary oedema."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"How can heart failure occur despite ejection fraction 60%?","options":["Systolic function is the only determinant of heart failure","The right ventricle replaces LV filling","The myocardium cannot experience pressure overload","The LV may be stiff and have impaired diastolic filling"],"answer":"D","explain":"The LV may be stiff and have impaired diastolic filling. HFpEF can result from reduced LV compliance and raised filling pressures despite preserved EF."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Why might an S4 sound be present?","options":["Mitral leaflet vegetation scratching the pericardium","Atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle","Very rapid passive filling of a dilated ventricle","Airway closure during expiration"],"answer":"B","explain":"Atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle. S4 reflects atrial contraction against reduced ventricular compliance."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"What does an elevated natriuretic peptide predominantly reflect?","options":["An absolute diagnosis of myocardial infarction","Ventricular wall stretch","Bronchial smooth-muscle constriction","Bacterial bloodstream infection"],"answer":"B","explain":"Ventricular wall stretch. BNP/NT-proBNP is released with myocardial wall stretch; elevation supports but is not specific for HF."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest immediate student response?","options":["Delay assessment until an echocardiogram is available","Ask a patient with severe distress to return next week","Start maximal unsupervised oral antihypertensives and leave","Call urgent senior/resuscitation help and support oxygenation/monitoring"],"answer":"D","explain":"Call urgent senior/resuscitation help and support oxygenation/monitoring. Acute hypoxaemia and pulmonary oedema require immediate senior-led stabilisation."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Arterial blood gas on air: PaO₂ 48 mmHg, PaCO₂ 31 mmHg, pH 7.47. Which primary problem is clear?","options":["Normal gas exchange","Severe hypoxaemia with respiratory alkalosis","Chronic compensated respiratory acidosis","Isolated metabolic acidosis"],"answer":"B","explain":"Severe hypoxaemia with respiratory alkalosis. Low PaO₂ indicates hypoxaemia and low CO₂ with raised pH indicates respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — What heart-failure category has preserved EF and impaired filling?","answer":"HFpEF","explain":"HFpEF. Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction commonly reflects diastolic dysfunction."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the left-ventricular wall response to chronic pressure overload.","answer":"Concentric hypertrophy","explain":"Concentric hypertrophy. Long-standing hypertension produces a thick, less compliant LV."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-07","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 71-year-old woman with hypertension presents with palpitations and fatigue. Pulse is irregularly irregular at 138/min and BP 126/78 mmHg. She has no chest pain or syncope. ECG shows no consistent P waves and irregular QRS intervals.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the most likely rhythm?","options":["Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Complete heart block","Regular atrial flutter with fixed conduction"],"answer":"A","explain":"Atrial fibrillation. Atrial fibrillation has no organised P waves and an irregularly irregular ventricular rhythm."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Why does atrial fibrillation increase ischaemic stroke risk?","options":["Stasis in the left atrium/appendage promotes thrombus","Obstruction of the carotid artery by airway mucus","Increased platelet production by the spleen","Acute breakdown of erythrocytes"],"answer":"A","explain":"Stasis in the left atrium/appendage promotes thrombus. Loss of coordinated atrial contraction favours thrombus formation and systemic embolisation."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which node principally determines how many rapid atrial impulses reach the ventricles?","options":["Sinoatrial node","Left bundle branch alone","Purkinje network","Atrioventricular node"],"answer":"D","explain":"Atrioventricular node. The AV node filters atrial impulses; rate-control drugs often modify AV conduction."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which clinical factor is relevant when assessing stroke prevention?","options":["Hair colour","History of hypertension","History of seasonal rhinitis only","Normal height"],"answer":"B","explain":"History of hypertension. Hypertension is a recognised component of clinical AF stroke-risk assessment."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"The patient suddenly becomes hypotensive, confused and has chest pain. What changes?","options":["Urgent senior emergency assessment for unstable tachyarrhythmia","Repeat an ECG in several weeks before acting","Give antihistamines first","Continue non-urgent outpatient review"],"answer":"A","explain":"Urgent senior emergency assessment for unstable tachyarrhythmia. Instability demands immediate senior-led assessment and possible synchronised cardioversion."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — ECG: absent discrete P waves, beat-to-beat variable R-R intervals, narrow QRS. Best ECG interpretation?","options":["Second-degree AV block Mobitz II","Sinus arrhythmia with normal P waves","Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response","Ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":"C","explain":"Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response. Irregular narrow complexes without organised P waves fit AF with rapid ventricular response."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the stroke mechanism caused by emboli originating within the heart.","answer":"Cardioembolism","explain":"Cardioembolism. Intracardiac thrombi can embolise into the cerebral circulation."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the left atrial structure where thrombi frequently form in AF.","answer":"Left atrial appendage","explain":"Left atrial appendage. Blood stasis in the appendage predisposes to thromboembolism."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-08","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 76-year-old man experiences recurrent fainting spells. BP is 78/44 mmHg and pulse 32/min. ECG shows regular P waves occurring independently of a slower regular broad-QRS escape rhythm. He is pale and confused.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which rhythm abnormality is most likely?","options":["Atrial fibrillation","First-degree AV block","Sinus tachycardia","Complete (third-degree) AV block"],"answer":"D","explain":"Complete (third-degree) AV block. Independent P waves and QRS complexes demonstrate AV dissociation due to complete heart block."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"What anatomical conduction problem underlies this ECG finding?","options":["Isolated increase in sinus-node firing","Excessive conduction through the AV node","Failure of atrial impulses to conduct to the ventricles","Rapid ventricular re-entry through accessory pathway"],"answer":"C","explain":"Failure of atrial impulses to conduct to the ventricles. In complete block, atrial depolarisations cannot reliably activate ventricles."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is syncope plausible with this rate?","options":["Heart block increases coronary oxygen extraction enough to prevent symptoms","Venous return becomes irrelevant to cardiac output","Severely reduced heart rate can decrease cardiac output and brain perfusion","Bradycardia always improves cerebral perfusion"],"answer":"C","explain":"Severely reduced heart rate can decrease cardiac output and brain perfusion. Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume; profound bradycardia may cause cerebral hypoperfusion."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which medication is commonly considered as a temporary supervised intervention for symptomatic bradycardia, although it may not work in high-grade block?","options":["Warfarin","Salbutamol","Aspirin","Atropine"],"answer":"D","explain":"Atropine. Atropine can increase SA/AV nodal activity but may be ineffective in infranodal complete block."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest action at this level?","options":["Give beta blockers for the low pulse","Arrange a routine appointment","Encourage walking to increase heart rate","Urgently summon resuscitation support and prepare for pacing assessment"],"answer":"D","explain":"Urgently summon resuscitation support and prepare for pacing assessment. Hypotension, confusion and complete block require emergency support and potential pacing."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — ECG strip shows atrial rate 92/min, ventricular rate 30/min with no fixed PR relationship. Interpret the conduction defect.","options":["Second-degree block with gradually lengthening PR","Normal sinus conduction","AV dissociation from complete heart block","Atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction"],"answer":"C","explain":"AV dissociation from complete heart block. Independent atrial and ventricular rates with absent P-to-QRS relation indicate third-degree block."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the conduction finding where P waves and QRS complexes have no consistent relationship.","answer":"AV dissociation","explain":"AV dissociation. Separate atrial and ventricular pacemakers generate independent rhythms."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the definitive device-based therapy often needed for persistent symptomatic complete AV block.","answer":"Pacemaker","explain":"Pacemaker. Permanent pacing is commonly indicated after specialist assessment of persistent block."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-09","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 12-year-old child develops fever, migrating pain and swelling of the knees and ankles three weeks after an untreated sore throat. Examination reveals tachycardia and a new apical holosystolic murmur. An antistreptolysin O titre is raised.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What unifying diagnosis should be considered?","options":["Kawasaki disease without cardiac changes","Viral bronchiolitis","Congenital isolated pulmonic stenosis","Acute rheumatic fever with carditis"],"answer":"D","explain":"Acute rheumatic fever with carditis. Migratory polyarthritis and carditis weeks after streptococcal pharyngitis strongly suggest acute rheumatic fever."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which process causes post-streptococcal cardiac injury?","options":["Congenital absence of valve leaflets","Molecular mimicry leading to cross-reactive immunity","Direct bacterial invasion of valves in every case","Antibody deficiency causing opportunistic infection"],"answer":"B","explain":"Molecular mimicry leading to cross-reactive immunity. Group A streptococcal antigens trigger immune responses that cross-react with host cardiac tissues."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which valve is most commonly affected by chronic rheumatic disease?","options":["Pulmonary valve","All valves equally and always","Tricuspid valve","Mitral valve"],"answer":"D","explain":"Mitral valve. Mitral involvement predominates in chronic rheumatic valvular disease."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"What accounts for a new pansystolic murmur during active carditis?","options":["Mitral regurgitation","A normal physiological split S2","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary venous obstruction"],"answer":"A","explain":"Mitral regurgitation. Acute rheumatic valvulitis commonly causes mitral regurgitation and an apical pansystolic murmur."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What long-term preventive principle reduces recurrent immune injury?","options":["Give routine antituberculosis prophylaxis","Secondary antibiotic prophylaxis as directed by the clinical team","Avoid all physical activity for life","Treat future sore throats only with antihistamines"],"answer":"B","explain":"Secondary antibiotic prophylaxis as directed by the clinical team. Secondary penicillin prophylaxis prevents recurrent group A streptococcal infection and attacks."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — ESR and CRP are raised; ASO titre is high. Echo shows new mitral regurgitation. Which interpretation is best?","options":["Mitral regurgitation excludes inflammatory disease","Only current bloodstream infection is established","Normal skeletal radiographs rule out rheumatic fever","Evidence supports preceding GAS infection with rheumatic carditis in this clinical context"],"answer":"D","explain":"Evidence supports preceding GAS infection with rheumatic carditis in this clinical context. Evidence of prior GAS plus compatible inflammation and echo valve disease supports acute rheumatic fever."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the organism implicated in the preceding pharyngitis.","answer":"Streptococcus pyogenes","explain":"Streptococcus pyogenes. Group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus triggers this immune sequela."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the diagnostic criteria framework used for acute rheumatic fever.","answer":"Jones criteria","explain":"Jones criteria. Jones criteria combine major/minor manifestations and evidence of preceding GAS infection."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-10","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 3-year-old boy with a known congenital murmur becomes blue and breathless during vigorous play. He instinctively squats, after which the colour improves. Examination shows a harsh ejection systolic murmur at the upper left sternal border and digital clubbing.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which congenital defect best unifies the episodic cyanosis and squatting?","options":["Tetralogy of Fallot","Isolated mild mitral regurgitation","Patent ductus arteriosus with isolated left-to-right shunt","Atrial septal defect without pulmonary obstruction"],"answer":"A","explain":"Tetralogy of Fallot. Tetralogy of Fallot causes episodic cyanosis and hypercyanotic spells that improve with squatting."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which structural combination is correct for this defect?","options":["ASD, pulmonary venous stenosis, LV hypertrophy and bicuspid aortic valve","Aortic coarctation, ASD, pulmonary oedema and normal RV","VSD, overriding aorta, RV outflow obstruction and RV hypertrophy","VSD, mitral stenosis, PDA and LA hypertrophy"],"answer":"C","explain":"VSD, overriding aorta, RV outflow obstruction and RV hypertrophy. The four components are VSD, overriding aorta, RV outflow obstruction and RV hypertrophy."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Why can squatting improve a hypercyanotic spell?","options":["It promotes closure of the VSD","It increases systemic vascular resistance and reduces right-to-left shunting","It reduces systemic arterial resistance","It acutely increases right-to-left shunting"],"answer":"B","explain":"It increases systemic vascular resistance and reduces right-to-left shunting. Squatting increases systemic vascular resistance and can reduce deoxygenated blood bypassing the lungs."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"What embryological developmental process is particularly relevant to this condition?","options":["Abnormal partitioning of the conotruncal outflow tract","Failure of neural tube closure","Failure of somite ossification","Premature closure of the foramen ovale only"],"answer":"A","explain":"Abnormal partitioning of the conotruncal outflow tract. Anterior displacement/malalignment of the outflow septum underlies TOF anatomy."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest response to a prolonged severe cyanotic spell?","options":["Administer an unsupervised diuretic","Treat as routine exercise-induced asthma only","Urgently summon paediatric/emergency help and support positioning and oxygenation","Delay care until routine congenital clinic"],"answer":"C","explain":"Urgently summon paediatric/emergency help and support positioning and oxygenation. Hypercyanotic spells can cause severe hypoxaemia and require urgent supervised management."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Pulse oximeter is 74% during a spell and rises to 88% after knee-to-chest positioning. What change is most likely?","options":["Increased flow across the obstructed right ventricular outflow is impossible","Reduced right-to-left shunting","Measurement proves a normal circulation","Immediate closure of the VSD"],"answer":"B","explain":"Reduced right-to-left shunting. Increasing systemic resistance can lessen the proportion of blood shunting from right to left."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the septal defect in tetralogy of Fallot.","answer":"VSD","explain":"VSD. A ventricular septal defect connects the ventricular chambers."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the chamber that develops hypertrophy in this condition.","answer":"Right ventricle","explain":"Right ventricle. Right ventricular outflow obstruction creates chronic right-ventricular pressure overload."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-11","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 33-year-old woman arrives after a road traffic collision. She is alert but anxious, with cool clammy skin, pulse 136/min, BP 82/48 mmHg and delayed capillary refill. There is a large open leg injury with active haemorrhage.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which type of shock is most likely?","options":["Hypovolaemic shock from blood loss","Cardiogenic shock from LV pump failure","Distributive shock from widespread vasodilation","Obstructive shock from tamponade"],"answer":"A","explain":"Hypovolaemic shock from blood loss. Acute haemorrhage depletes circulating volume, lowering venous return and preload."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the earliest central haemodynamic consequence of major volume loss?","options":["Reduced venous return and ventricular preload","Increased systemic capillary oncotic pressure","Increased red-cell mass","Increased ventricular end-diastolic filling"],"answer":"A","explain":"Reduced venous return and ventricular preload. Loss of circulating volume reduces cardiac filling and stroke volume."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What causes the cool clammy skin?","options":["Parasympathetic vasodilation","Increased interstitial fluid return","Reflex sympathetic peripheral vasoconstriction","Immediate restoration of peripheral perfusion"],"answer":"C","explain":"Reflex sympathetic peripheral vasoconstriction. Sympathetic vasoconstriction diverts flow toward vital organs."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Why can lactate increase in shock?","options":["Lactate uniquely diagnoses cardiogenic shock","Normal oxygen delivery prevents glucose utilisation","Lactate is secreted only by the kidneys","Tissue hypoperfusion promotes anaerobic glycolysis"],"answer":"D","explain":"Tissue hypoperfusion promotes anaerobic glycolysis. Poor oxygen delivery increases anaerobic metabolism and lactate production."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immediate step is most important in addition to resuscitation-team activation?","options":["Delay care for routine fasting tests","Encourage ambulation to restore BP","Provide an oral fluid challenge as sole treatment","Prompt haemorrhage control with appropriate senior-led resuscitation"],"answer":"D","explain":"Prompt haemorrhage control with appropriate senior-led resuscitation. Stopping external bleeding and restoring effective perfusion are time-critical."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — BP 78/46 mmHg, pulse 144/min, lactate 5.8 mmol/L, urine output very low. Interpretation?","options":["Anaphylaxis is confirmed by low BP alone","Severe ongoing hypoperfusion is likely","Adequate end-organ perfusion is proven","Only an isolated high lactate with no clinical significance"],"answer":"B","explain":"Severe ongoing hypoperfusion is likely. Hypotension, tachycardia, high lactate and oliguria collectively indicate shock."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the cardiac filling variable reduced by haemorrhage.","answer":"Preload","explain":"Preload. Decreased venous return lowers end-diastolic ventricular filling."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the primary circulating catecholamine released by sympathetic nerve endings.","answer":"Noradrenaline","explain":"Noradrenaline. Noradrenaline mediates peripheral vasoconstriction during compensation."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-12","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 43-year-old man develops acute breathlessness and syncope soon after a penetrating chest injury. BP is 76/42 mmHg, pulse 130/min, neck veins are visibly distended and heart sounds are very quiet. Breath sounds are present bilaterally.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which cause of shock is especially concerning?","options":["Acute isolated asthma","Chronic hypertension","Cardiac tamponade","Simple dehydration"],"answer":"C","explain":"Cardiac tamponade. Hypotension, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds after trauma suggest pericardial tamponade."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism reduces cardiac output?","options":["Primary loss of myocardial contractile proteins from infection","Increased left-ventricular preload","Fluid around the heart limits diastolic ventricular filling","Isolated systemic vasodilation alone"],"answer":"C","explain":"Fluid around the heart limits diastolic ventricular filling. Pericardial pressure compresses the heart, obstructs filling and reduces stroke volume."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which bedside investigation could rapidly support the diagnosis during resuscitation?","options":["Elective colonoscopy","Point-of-care focused cardiac ultrasound","Routine spirometry","Thyroid uptake scan"],"answer":"B","explain":"Point-of-care focused cardiac ultrasound. Bedside echocardiography can demonstrate pericardial fluid and signs of tamponade."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"How does this differ from uncomplicated hypovolaemic haemorrhagic shock?","options":["Haemorrhage always causes bradycardia","Raised JVP despite hypotension suggests impaired cardiac filling/outflow","Tamponade always causes severe wheeze","All shock states cause low JVP"],"answer":"B","explain":"Raised JVP despite hypotension suggests impaired cardiac filling/outflow. High venous pressure with shock is a clue to impaired filling rather than only volume loss."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the correct student-level response?","options":["Perform an unsupervised invasive drain","Immediately call the trauma/resuscitation team for urgent intervention","Wait for a formal outpatient echo","Treat with oral antibiotics only"],"answer":"B","explain":"Immediately call the trauma/resuscitation team for urgent intervention. Potential tamponade after trauma requires immediate specialist resuscitation and intervention."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Ultrasound shows a large pericardial effusion with right-atrial collapse during diastole. Most relevant implication?","options":["Obstructive circulatory compromise from tamponade is likely","The effusion is unlikely relevant to hypotension","Acute coronary thrombosis is proven","Normal filling is confirmed"],"answer":"A","explain":"Obstructive circulatory compromise from tamponade is likely. Right-sided chamber collapse in a hypotensive patient supports haemodynamically significant tamponade."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the clinical triad of hypotension, muffled heart sounds and elevated JVP.","answer":"Beck triad","explain":"Beck triad. Beck triad is a classic but not universally present tamponade pattern."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the physiological cardiac phase most directly restricted by tamponade.","answer":"Diastole","explain":"Diastole. External compression primarily limits ventricular diastolic filling."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-13","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 66-year-old man with dysuria for four days arrives febrile and confused. Temperature 39.3°C, pulse 125/min, respiratory rate 30/min, BP 84/48 mmHg and oxygen saturation 93%. His extremities are initially warm. Urine appears cloudy.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which syndrome must be considered urgently?","options":["Stable essential hypertension","Uncomplicated cystitis without systemic effects","Primary panic disorder","Sepsis with possible septic shock"],"answer":"D","explain":"Sepsis with possible septic shock. Suspected infection with acute organ dysfunction and hypotension raises concern for sepsis and shock."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism explains warm skin and hypotension early in septic distributive shock?","options":["Complete loss of total blood volume from bleeding","Mechanical obstruction of the aortic valve","Diffuse vasodilation lowering systemic vascular resistance","Increased red-cell count"],"answer":"C","explain":"Diffuse vasodilation lowering systemic vascular resistance. Inflammatory mediators and altered vascular tone lower resistance and can make skin warm."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What does new confusion suggest in this context?","options":["Guaranteed drug toxicity","A diagnosis of stroke without further assessment","Possible acute organ dysfunction from poor perfusion/inflammation","Normal healthy ageing"],"answer":"C","explain":"Possible acute organ dysfunction from poor perfusion/inflammation. Altered mental status may indicate sepsis-associated brain dysfunction/hypoperfusion."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"What is an appropriate early supervised action?","options":["Prompt urgent assessment, cultures when feasible and timely antibiotics/resuscitation","Administer antihypertensives for the low BP","Delay all treatment until final urine culture","Discharge when the fever falls briefly"],"answer":"A","explain":"Prompt urgent assessment, cultures when feasible and timely antibiotics/resuscitation. Early senior-led sepsis management prioritises recognition, sampling when feasible without delay, antibiotics and perfusion support."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which microbiological organism commonly causes urinary-source Gram-negative sepsis?","options":["Escherichia coli","Epstein-Barr virus","Mycobacterium leprae","Candida auris in every case"],"answer":"A","explain":"Escherichia coli. E. coli is a common cause of urinary tract infection and urosepsis."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Lactate 5.0 mmol/L, creatinine 212 µmol/L (baseline 86), BP 82/46. Best interpretation?","options":["Isolated dehydration is proven as the only cause","Acute kidney injury and hypoperfusion make this high risk","Sepsis is excluded because creatinine is elevated","Normal circulation with isolated fever"],"answer":"B","explain":"Acute kidney injury and hypoperfusion make this high risk. High lactate, AKI and hypotension indicate significant organ compromise requiring emergency care."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the circulatory shock category typically associated with vasodilation in sepsis.","answer":"Distributive shock","explain":"Distributive shock. Sepsis often produces pathologic systemic vasodilation."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the common Gram-negative urinary pathogen in this scenario.","answer":"Escherichia coli","explain":"Escherichia coli. E. coli is a major cause of ascending UTIs and bacteraemia."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-cvs-14","paper":"Paper 1","module":"CVS","subject":"CVS","scenario":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension develops sudden right facial droop and weakness of his right arm while eating breakfast. His speech is slurred and his family confirms onset 25 minutes earlier. BP 176/96 mmHg, glucose 5.6 mmol/L.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature most strongly suggests acute stroke?","options":["Sudden onset of focal neurological deficit","Mild tachycardia","A single history of smoking","Chronic knee pain"],"answer":"A","explain":"Sudden onset of focal neurological deficit. Abrupt focal deficit is classic for cerebrovascular injury."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which intracranial vascular territory often causes dominant hemispheric face/arm weakness and language disturbance?","options":["Posterior inferior cerebellar artery only","Anterior spinal artery","Middle cerebral artery","Ophthalmic artery only"],"answer":"C","explain":"Middle cerebral artery. MCA territory supplies much of the lateral cortex controlling face/arm motor function and dominant language areas."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is a bedside glucose check vital early in evaluation?","options":["High glucose rules out stroke","Glucose identifies the exact occluded artery","Hypoglycaemia can mimic focal neurological injury","Normal glucose confirms intracranial haemorrhage"],"answer":"C","explain":"Hypoglycaemia can mimic focal neurological injury. Hypoglycaemia can cause stroke-like neurological symptoms and is rapidly reversible."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"What investigation is essential to distinguish haemorrhagic from ischaemic stroke?","options":["Spirometry","Routine chest X-ray","Urgent non-contrast CT brain","Abdominal ultrasound"],"answer":"C","explain":"Urgent non-contrast CT brain. Brain imaging is needed to identify haemorrhage before stroke-specific interventions."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What should a GEMP1 student prioritise?","options":["Lower BP rapidly to normal without senior input","Give aspirin before brain imaging without supervision","Activate urgent stroke-team assessment and document last-known-well time","Wait overnight to see if weakness resolves"],"answer":"C","explain":"Activate urgent stroke-team assessment and document last-known-well time. Immediate stroke-pathway activation and accurate timing preserve options for time-sensitive treatment."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Non-contrast head CT shows no haemorrhage; the deficit persists. Which statement is most appropriate?","options":["The patient is safe to discharge","CT excludes all early ischaemic strokes","Haemorrhagic stroke is established","Ischaemic stroke remains possible and requires immediate specialist pathway assessment"],"answer":"D","explain":"Ischaemic stroke remains possible and requires immediate specialist pathway assessment. Early ischaemic CT can appear normal; absence of bleed does not exclude acute ischaemia."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the large brain artery commonly involved in lateral-hemisphere face/arm deficits.","answer":"Middle cerebral artery","explain":"Middle cerebral artery. MCA infarction often causes face/arm weakness and aphasia in a dominant hemisphere."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — What acronym is used for face, arm and speech stroke recognition with time urgency?","answer":"FAST","explain":"FAST. FAST is a common public stroke-recognition mnemonic."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-01","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 20-year-old student reports episodic chest tightness, dry cough and wheeze, especially after running and at night. She has eczema and allergic rhinitis. Between episodes her examination is normal. In clinic she has an audible expiratory wheeze.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which characteristic best distinguishes asthma from fixed obstructive disease?","options":["Variable respiratory symptoms with variable expiratory airflow limitation","Unvarying progressive obstruction without exacerbations","Wheeze only with fever","Persistent daily productive cough in every patient"],"answer":"A","explain":"Variable respiratory symptoms with variable expiratory airflow limitation. Asthma is characterised by variable symptoms and variable airflow limitation."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which airway process is central to asthma pathophysiology?","options":["Eosinophilic/Type 2 airway inflammation in many patients","Systemic venous thrombosis","Primary pleural fibrosis","Loss of all alveolar septa in every patient"],"answer":"A","explain":"Eosinophilic/Type 2 airway inflammation in many patients. Type 2 immune inflammation involving eosinophils, mast cells and cytokines is common in allergic asthma."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which effect explains immediate relief from inhaled salbutamol?","options":["Beta-2 receptor activation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle","Blocking histamine H1 receptors in all airways","Reducing pulmonary artery thrombus load","Killing respiratory bacteria"],"answer":"A","explain":"Beta-2 receptor activation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. Beta-2 agonists increase cAMP in airway smooth muscle, producing bronchodilation."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which medication class targets underlying airway inflammation for long-term control?","options":["Routine antibiotics","Anticoagulants","Long-term oral decongestants alone","Inhaled corticosteroids"],"answer":"D","explain":"Inhaled corticosteroids. ICS suppress airway inflammation and reduce exacerbation risk."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is an important counselling point when inhaled corticosteroids are prescribed?","options":["Avoid all physical activity","Rinse mouth after steroid inhalation and review inhaler technique","Use oral antibiotics after every dose","Stop every inhaler once wheeze improves"],"answer":"B","explain":"Rinse mouth after steroid inhalation and review inhaler technique. Rinsing and correct technique reduce local adverse effects, including oral candidiasis."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — FEV1 is 2.0 L before and 2.4 L after bronchodilator: increase 400 mL (20%). What is most likely?","options":["Meaningful bronchodilator reversibility supporting asthma","No change in airflow","An isolated restrictive defect","Definite pulmonary embolism"],"answer":"A","explain":"Meaningful bronchodilator reversibility supporting asthma. An increase in FEV1 of 400 mL and 20% is significant bronchodilator responsiveness."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the short-acting beta-2 agonist used for rapid bronchodilation in this scenario.","answer":"Salbutamol","explain":"Salbutamol. Salbutamol is a commonly used short-acting inhaled bronchodilator."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the inflammatory white blood cell commonly increased in allergic asthma.","answer":"Eosinophil","explain":"Eosinophil. Eosinophils are major effector cells in type-2 airway inflammation."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-02","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 26-year-old woman with asthma arrives unable to finish sentences. Her respiratory rate is 34/min and pulse 132/min. She has used her reliever repeatedly without relief. Initially loud wheezing becomes barely audible while her consciousness begins to fluctuate.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is a quiet or silent chest particularly dangerous here?","options":["It always means panic rather than respiratory disease","It may indicate critically reduced airflow despite worsening obstruction","It confirms pneumonia rather than asthma","It proves resolution of airway narrowing"],"answer":"B","explain":"It may indicate critically reduced airflow despite worsening obstruction. When airflow becomes critically low, wheeze can disappear even as obstruction worsens."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism most directly causes acute airflow obstruction?","options":["Alveolar bleeding from anticoagulation","Pleural fluid due solely to low albumin","Obstruction of the superior vena cava","Bronchial smooth-muscle constriction with mucosal oedema and mucus"],"answer":"D","explain":"Bronchial smooth-muscle constriction with mucosal oedema and mucus. Bronchospasm, inflammation/oedema and mucus plugs narrow the intrathoracic airways."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What acid-base trend is especially ominous if a previously low PaCO2 rises towards normal in a tiring patient?","options":["Sudden spontaneous pneumothorax is confirmed","Full resolution of the attack","A guaranteed metabolic alkalosis","Loss of effective ventilation with impending respiratory failure"],"answer":"D","explain":"Loss of effective ventilation with impending respiratory failure. Rising CO2 during severe asthma can indicate respiratory muscle exhaustion and failure to ventilate."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immediate action is safest for a student?","options":["Urgently request senior emergency/critical-care help and assist protocol-driven treatment","Discharge when wheeze becomes quieter","Continue observation without escalation","Give sedatives to reduce respiratory effort"],"answer":"A","explain":"Urgently request senior emergency/critical-care help and assist protocol-driven treatment. Altered consciousness and silent chest are life-threatening red flags requiring immediate escalation."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which medication is generally added early in a severe asthma exacerbation alongside rapid bronchodilators?","options":["Oral antihistamine as sole therapy","Long-term diuretic without assessment","Prophylactic anticoagulation to reverse wheeze","Systemic corticosteroid"],"answer":"D","explain":"Systemic corticosteroid. Systemic steroids reduce airway inflammation and relapse although they do not act instantly."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Initial ABG: pH 7.48, PaCO2 29 mmHg. Later: pH 7.34, PaCO2 47 mmHg and PaO2 53 mmHg despite treatment. Best interpretation?","options":["Improving ventilation is proven","Uncomplicated respiratory alkalosis remains stable","Impending ventilatory failure with significant hypoxaemia","A normal late PaCO2 excludes severe obstruction"],"answer":"C","explain":"Impending ventilatory failure with significant hypoxaemia. CO2 rise with acidaemia and hypoxaemia after initial hyperventilation signals exhaustion and respiratory failure."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the prolonged inability to reverse a severe asthma attack using usual therapy.","answer":"Status asthmaticus","explain":"Status asthmaticus. Status asthmaticus refers to a severe persistent asthma exacerbation."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Which gas has an arterial partial pressure represented by PaCO2?","answer":"Carbon dioxide","explain":"Carbon dioxide. PaCO2 reflects alveolar ventilation relative to CO2 production."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-03","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 65-year-old man with 40 pack-years of smoking has progressive exertional dyspnoea and a daily productive cough. He has a barrel-shaped chest and prolonged expiration. Chest radiograph suggests hyperinflation.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which spirometric pattern most strongly supports COPD?","options":["Increased FEV1/FVC after bronchodilator above normal in every case","Isolated normal FEV1 with normal ratio","Post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.70","Pure restriction with low total lung capacity only"],"answer":"C","explain":"Post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.70. Persistent post-bronchodilator airflow obstruction is central to COPD diagnosis; the notes use ratio below 0.70."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which process best explains emphysema-related airflow limitation?","options":["Primary pulmonary vascular embolism","Excess alveolar surfactant increases expiratory flow","Fibrosis of the parietal pleura in every case","Destruction of alveolar walls reduces elastic recoil"],"answer":"D","explain":"Destruction of alveolar walls reduces elastic recoil. Loss of alveolar attachments/elastic recoil promotes small-airway collapse during exhalation."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which exposure is the largest modifiable risk factor in this patient?","options":["Cold drinks","Cigarette smoke","Low dietary iron","Indoor pollen only"],"answer":"B","explain":"Cigarette smoke. Long-term tobacco smoke exposure causes chronic airway and parenchymal injury."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which inherited condition should be considered in younger patients with basal-predominant emphysema?","options":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Haemophilia A","G6PD deficiency","Cystinuria"],"answer":"A","explain":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency increases protease-mediated alveolar damage."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which long-term intervention most reliably slows smoking-related COPD progression?","options":["Routine antibiotics every day for all patients","Avoidance of every form of exercise","Inhaled reliever alone without risk reduction","Smoking cessation support"],"answer":"D","explain":"Smoking cessation support. Smoking cessation reduces continuing lung injury and slows FEV1 decline."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Pre-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC 0.54; post-bronchodilator 0.58. How should this be read?","options":["Isolated restrictive disease established","Full reversal to normal airflow","Persistent obstructive ventilatory defect","Normal spirometry despite symptoms"],"answer":"C","explain":"Persistent obstructive ventilatory defect. A post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.58 remains obstructed."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the enzyme that damages alveolar elastin when alpha-1 antitrypsin protection is deficient.","answer":"Neutrophil elastase","explain":"Neutrophil elastase. Unopposed neutrophil elastase promotes alveolar wall destruction."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Which spirometric ratio is used to identify airflow obstruction?","answer":"FEV1/FVC","explain":"FEV1/FVC. The forced expiratory volume in one second to forced vital capacity ratio is reduced in obstruction."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-04","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 70-year-old man with established COPD has increasing breathlessness and purulent sputum. He is drowsy, cyanosed and wheezing. BP 132/76 mmHg, respiratory rate 30/min, oxygen saturation 82% on room air. He also has bilateral ankle oedema and an elevated JVP.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which presentation best indicates an acute COPD exacerbation?","options":["Isolated swollen knee","Acute worsening dyspnoea with increased sputum volume/purulence","New asymptomatic hypertension only","Stable exercise intolerance unchanged for months"],"answer":"B","explain":"Acute worsening dyspnoea with increased sputum volume/purulence. Acute deterioration in breathlessness and sputum suggests exacerbation, sometimes infectious."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Why can chronic COPD cause cor pulmonale?","options":["Venous blood becomes fully oxygenated","Chronic hypoxaemia produces pulmonary vasoconstriction and RV pressure overload","Left coronary thrombosis directly destroys the RV in every case","Pleural pressure becomes zero in all patients"],"answer":"B","explain":"Chronic hypoxaemia produces pulmonary vasoconstriction and RV pressure overload. Long-term hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction can raise pulmonary artery pressure and cause right-heart failure."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What initial oxygen strategy is appropriate while urgently evaluating hypercapnia risk?","options":["Titrate controlled oxygen toward a target of 88–92% in patients at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure","Use oxygen only after the patient loses consciousness","Avoid oxygen regardless of profound hypoxaemia","Give unrestricted maximal oxygen and avoid monitoring"],"answer":"A","explain":"Titrate controlled oxygen toward a target of 88–92% in patients at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure. Controlled oxygen treats hypoxaemia while limiting worsening CO2 retention in at-risk COPD."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which investigation directly assesses CO2 retention and acid-base status?","options":["Serum amylase","Arterial blood gas","D-dimer alone","Urine protein dipstick"],"answer":"B","explain":"Arterial blood gas. ABG identifies hypoxaemia, hypercapnia and respiratory acidosis."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"The patient becomes more drowsy with severe acidaemia. Best action?","options":["Send home after repeat inhaler","Treat with an antihistamine alone","Stop oxygen and wait","Urgent senior respiratory/resuscitation assessment for ventilatory support"],"answer":"D","explain":"Urgent senior respiratory/resuscitation assessment for ventilatory support. Altered consciousness and hypercapnic acidosis require immediate escalation and assessment for assisted ventilation."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — ABG on controlled oxygen: pH 7.27, PaCO2 65 mmHg, HCO3− 29 mmol/L. What is the principal acid-base disorder?","options":["Respiratory alkalosis","Normal acid-base balance","Metabolic alkalosis with complete compensation","Hypercapnic respiratory acidosis with partial metabolic compensation"],"answer":"D","explain":"Hypercapnic respiratory acidosis with partial metabolic compensation. Low pH with high CO2 indicates respiratory acidosis; elevated bicarbonate suggests chronic renal compensation."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the pulmonary vascular response to low alveolar oxygen levels.","answer":"Hypoxic vasoconstriction","explain":"Hypoxic vasoconstriction. Regional pulmonary arterioles constrict in response to low alveolar oxygen."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the gas whose retention causes type II respiratory failure.","answer":"Carbon dioxide","explain":"Carbon dioxide. Hypercapnic respiratory failure is characterised by elevated PaCO2."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-05","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 38-year-old teacher presents with abrupt fever, pleuritic right-sided chest pain and cough producing rust-coloured sputum. Temperature 39.1°C, pulse 112/min, respiratory rate 28/min. Examination reveals bronchial breathing and dullness to percussion in the right lower chest.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which diagnosis best fits the focal examination findings?","options":["Stable asthma without infection","Community-acquired lobar pneumonia","Isolated allergic rhinitis","Acute pericarditis"],"answer":"B","explain":"Community-acquired lobar pneumonia. Fever, productive cough, dullness and bronchial breathing suggest alveolar consolidation."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which organism classically causes community-acquired lobar pneumonia?","options":["Candida albicans in a healthy adult","Mycobacterium leprae","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Plasmodium falciparum"],"answer":"C","explain":"Streptococcus pneumoniae. Streptococcus pneumoniae is a classic cause of typical lobar pneumonia."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Why is percussion dull over the affected region?","options":["Alveolar air is replaced by inflammatory exudate","Excess nitrogen accumulates in alveoli","Loss of pleural innervation","Pulmonary blood flow becomes zero in every patient"],"answer":"A","explain":"Alveolar air is replaced by inflammatory exudate. Fluid-filled consolidated alveoli transmit a dull percussion note."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which radiographic pattern supports this diagnosis?","options":["Dense focal air-space opacity with possible air bronchograms","Enlarged thymus in the adult","Isolated bilateral hyperinflation only","A normal radiograph must be present"],"answer":"A","explain":"Dense focal air-space opacity with possible air bronchograms. A lobar air-space opacity with air bronchograms reflects consolidation."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is an appropriate initial student role?","options":["Instruct patient to ignore tachypnoea","Treat every patient with antifungal therapy only","Defer assessment until routine sputum cultures return","Evaluate severity and promptly involve the supervising team for antibiotics and supportive care"],"answer":"D","explain":"Evaluate severity and promptly involve the supervising team for antibiotics and supportive care. Recognise CAP and severity, support oxygenation when needed and initiate timely supervised care."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — WBC 17.5 ×10^9/L (neutrophils 89%), CRP elevated, CXR right lower-lobe consolidation. Which interpretation fits?","options":["Normal inflammatory markers","Confirmed pulmonary embolism","Likely acute bacterial lower respiratory infection","Uncomplicated isolated rhinitis"],"answer":"C","explain":"Likely acute bacterial lower respiratory infection. Neutrophilia and focal radiographic consolidation are compatible with bacterial pneumonia."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the alveolar process causing bronchial breathing and dullness.","answer":"Consolidation","explain":"Consolidation. Alveoli fill with inflammatory material and conduct breath sounds differently."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the granulocyte typically prominent in bacterial alveolar exudates.","answer":"Neutrophil","explain":"Neutrophil. Neutrophils accumulate during acute bacterial inflammation."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-06","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 78-year-old man has a seizure followed by vomiting while lying supine. Two days later he is febrile, tachypnoeic and coughs. A chest X-ray shows dependent posterior right-upper-lobe and superior right-lower-lobe opacities. He is struggling to swallow safely.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism best explains this respiratory illness?","options":["Airway constriction from seasonal pollen only","Pulmonary fibrosis from years of silica exposure","Aspiration of oropharyngeal/gastric material into dependent lung","Primary pleural haemorrhage"],"answer":"C","explain":"Aspiration of oropharyngeal/gastric material into dependent lung. Reduced airway protection during seizure/vomiting allows aspirated material to enter gravity-dependent lung segments."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which clinical factor increased aspiration risk?","options":["History of eczema","Elevated platelet count","Normal oxygen saturation months earlier","Impaired consciousness during the seizure"],"answer":"D","explain":"Impaired consciousness during the seizure. Loss of airway protective reflexes predisposes to aspiration."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pathogen group can contribute to aspiration-related bacterial infection?","options":["Only helminths","Only dermatophytes","Only Plasmodium species","Mixed oral flora including anaerobes"],"answer":"D","explain":"Mixed oral flora including anaerobes. Oral anaerobes and other organisms may contribute depending on clinical context."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"What does failure of safe swallowing raise concern for before discharge?","options":["Complete prevention of lower respiratory infection","Guaranteed normal cough reflex","Recurrent aspiration and need for swallow assessment","No need for multidisciplinary care"],"answer":"C","explain":"Recurrent aspiration and need for swallow assessment. Swallow assessment and nutrition/aspiration planning reduce repeated episodes."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature is a reason to urgently escalate a pneumonia case?","options":["Resolution of fever with normal observations","Severe hypoxaemia or new confusion","Mild sore throat alone in a stable patient","Appetite improvement"],"answer":"B","explain":"Severe hypoxaemia or new confusion. Hypoxaemia, sepsis and altered mental status are danger signs."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — SpO2 86%, respiratory rate 34/min and BP 88/52 mmHg. What is the safest interpretation?","options":["Mild stable outpatient disease","Normal findings after a seizure","Severe infection with respiratory/circulatory compromise requiring urgent support","Evidence that bacteria cannot be present"],"answer":"C","explain":"Severe infection with respiratory/circulatory compromise requiring urgent support. Hypoxaemia, tachypnoea and hypotension signify high-risk pneumonia or aspiration-related respiratory disease."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the path by which inhaled gastric contents enter the lungs.","answer":"Aspiration","explain":"Aspiration. Material can enter the lower airway when protective reflexes fail."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the clinical assessment required for unsafe swallowing.","answer":"Swallow assessment","explain":"Swallow assessment. Speech-language/swallow assessment helps identify aspiration risk."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-07","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 29-year-old man living in a crowded household in Johannesburg reports six weeks of cough, evening fevers, night sweats, weight loss and loss of appetite. A household contact recently completed tuberculosis treatment. He has upper-zone crackles and an HIV test is pending.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which infection should be prioritised in the differential?","options":["Acute uncomplicated allergic rhinitis","Uncomplicated acute viral laryngitis","Isolated heart failure with no infectious possibility","Pulmonary tuberculosis"],"answer":"D","explain":"Pulmonary tuberculosis. Chronic cough, constitutional symptoms and TB contact strongly suggest pulmonary TB."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which immune mechanism is important in containing Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","options":["Isolated histamine release","IgE-induced mast cell degranulation","Antibody blockade of acetylcholine receptors","Th1 CD4-cell activation of macrophages"],"answer":"D","explain":"Th1 CD4-cell activation of macrophages. Cell-mediated Th1 immunity activates macrophages and supports granuloma formation."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pathologic pattern is characteristic of TB granulomas?","options":["Diffuse atheromatous plaques","Caseating granulomatous inflammation","Purely eosinophilic asthma-like plugs","Coagulative necrosis without inflammation in every lesion"],"answer":"B","explain":"Caseating granulomatous inflammation. TB classically produces granulomas with central caseous necrosis."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which initial specimen/test is most appropriate for pulmonary TB investigation in this setting?","options":["Throat culture only for group A streptococci","An ECG instead of respiratory sampling","Sputum molecular NAAT such as Xpert MTB/RIF","Routine serum IgE alone"],"answer":"C","explain":"Sputum molecular NAAT such as Xpert MTB/RIF. Sputum NAAT rapidly detects MTB and can identify rifampicin resistance markers."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which public-health action complements individual clinical treatment?","options":["Do not inform any healthcare team","Ignore crowded household risk","Contact investigation and appropriate infection-prevention measures","Give all adult contacts antibiotics without assessment"],"answer":"C","explain":"Contact investigation and appropriate infection-prevention measures. Close contact screening and infection control reduce undetected transmission."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Sputum Xpert: MTB detected; rifampicin resistance not detected. What does it mean?","options":["Microbiological evidence of TB with no rifampicin resistance detected by this assay","Only latent infection is established","No active TB is possible","The result demonstrates full susceptibility to every TB drug"],"answer":"A","explain":"Microbiological evidence of TB with no rifampicin resistance detected by this assay. MTB detection supports pulmonary TB; rifampicin resistance was not found, but susceptibility to all drugs is not proved."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the necrosis type typical of TB granulomas.","answer":"Caseous necrosis","explain":"Caseous necrosis. Central cheese-like acellular necrosis is a hallmark TB pathology feature."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Which anti-TB drug is abbreviated RIF in Xpert MTB/RIF?","answer":"Rifampicin","explain":"Rifampicin. The assay assesses a genetic marker associated with rifampicin resistance."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-08","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 35-year-old woman with persistent fever, weight loss and pleuritic chest pain has a left pleural effusion. Chest ultrasound confirms fluid. She has been in contact with a relative treated for TB. A supervised thoracentesis yields clear yellow fluid.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which underlying mechanism can cause a TB-related pleural effusion?","options":["Inflammatory pleural response increasing capillary permeability","Reduced airway smooth-muscle tone","Chronic right ventricular outflow obstruction alone","Simple excessive sodium intake"],"answer":"A","explain":"Inflammatory pleural response increasing capillary permeability. TB pleuritis produces an inflammatory protein-rich pleural exudate."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which examination pattern most supports a pleural effusion?","options":["Isolated apical wheeze with normal percussion","Diffuse upper-airway stridor only","Hyperresonance and absent fremitus in all lung fields","Stony dullness and reduced breath sounds over fluid"],"answer":"D","explain":"Stony dullness and reduced breath sounds over fluid. Fluid dampens sound transmission and causes stony dullness and reduced breath sounds."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What predominates in many uncomplicated TB pleural exudates?","options":["Platelets","Eosinophils in every case","Pure erythrocytes","Lymphocytes"],"answer":"D","explain":"Lymphocytes. Tuberculous pleuritis commonly causes a lymphocyte-predominant exudate."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Why may pleural fluid microbiology be less sensitive than sputum or tissue studies?","options":["All pleural fluid is sterile by definition","Mycobacteria never involve the pleura","Pleural TB is often paucibacillary","NAAT detects antibodies rather than DNA"],"answer":"C","explain":"Pleural TB is often paucibacillary. Pleural TB often contains relatively few organisms, reducing microbiologic test sensitivity."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the safest student-level approach to a significant unexplained effusion?","options":["Give anticoagulants immediately without evaluation","Assume all effusions are due to heart failure","Perform an unsupervised drainage procedure","Seek senior supervised evaluation, imaging and appropriate fluid investigations"],"answer":"D","explain":"Seek senior supervised evaluation, imaging and appropriate fluid investigations. Effusion assessment includes supervised thoracentesis where indicated and diagnosis of the underlying cause."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Pleural protein 42 g/L, serum protein 70 g/L. Pleural/serum protein ratio = 0.60. Interpret using Light criteria.","options":["Confirms malignancy rather than TB","Pleural fluid is normal","Meets the protein criterion for an exudate","Definitively transudative from protein alone"],"answer":"C","explain":"Meets the protein criterion for an exudate. Protein ratio 42/70 = 0.60, greater than 0.5, so it meets an exudative criterion."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the classification for a protein-rich inflammatory pleural effusion.","answer":"Exudate","explain":"Exudate. Increased pleural permeability creates an exudative effusion."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Which pleural-fluid white cell often predominates in tuberculous effusion?","answer":"Lymphocytes","explain":"Lymphocytes. TB pleuritis typically shows lymphocyte predominance."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-09","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 5-month-old infant develops rhinorrhoea and cough followed by wheeze, fast breathing and poor feeding. Examination shows diffuse crackles and wheeze with subcostal recession. A sibling has had a recent cold. Temperature is 37.8°C.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which condition best explains this typical infant illness?","options":["Acute viral bronchiolitis","Primary pulmonary embolism","Chronic adult COPD","Rheumatic heart disease"],"answer":"A","explain":"Acute viral bronchiolitis. Bronchiolitis is a common viral lower-airway illness in infants with wheeze, crackles and increased work of breathing."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which virus commonly causes infant bronchiolitis?","options":["Human immunodeficiency virus alone","Hepatitis B virus","Respiratory syncytial virus","Varicella-zoster virus in every case"],"answer":"C","explain":"Respiratory syncytial virus. RSV is an important cause of bronchiolitis in infants."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which structural feature makes infants vulnerable to obstruction?","options":["Failure of pulmonary venous return in every child","Narrow bronchioles that are easily obstructed by oedema and mucus","Very large adult-sized airway diameters","Complete absence of mucociliary clearance from birth"],"answer":"B","explain":"Narrow bronchioles that are easily obstructed by oedema and mucus. Even small increases in airway-wall thickness markedly increase resistance in tiny infant airways."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature especially suggests need for urgent clinical assessment?","options":["Apnoea or inability to feed adequately","Brief self-limiting cough without distress","Mild clear rhinorrhoea in a thriving infant","Normal activity and hydration"],"answer":"A","explain":"Apnoea or inability to feed adequately. Apnoea, dehydration/feeding failure and severe work of breathing are important danger signs."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is a core treatment principle in otherwise uncomplicated bronchiolitis?","options":["Unsupervised bronchoscopy","Routine antibiotics for every infant","Routine high-dose systemic steroids for all","Supportive care with hydration and oxygen when indicated"],"answer":"D","explain":"Supportive care with hydration and oxygen when indicated. Supportive care is foundational; antibiotics and bronchodilators are not routinely indicated for typical bronchiolitis."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — SpO2 has fallen from 95% to 88% on room air, respiratory rate is 62/min and there are apnoeic pauses. Interpretation?","options":["Severe infant respiratory disease requiring urgent escalation","Normal infant vital signs","Confirmed uncomplicated rhinitis only","Proof of bacterial pneumonia"],"answer":"A","explain":"Severe infant respiratory disease requiring urgent escalation. Hypoxaemia, marked tachypnoea and apnoea warrant urgent hospital-level assessment."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the potentially dangerous temporary cessation of breathing in a young infant.","answer":"Apnoea","explain":"Apnoea. Apnoea in bronchiolitis is a serious danger sign, especially in young infants."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the small airway primarily inflamed in bronchiolitis.","answer":"Bronchiole","explain":"Bronchiole. Bronchiolar inflammation and mucus narrow the distal airways."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-10","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 31-year-old teacher has ten days of blocked nose, purulent nasal drainage and worsening facial pressure after an initial upper respiratory infection. Symptoms briefly improved before deteriorating again. He has unilateral maxillary tenderness and no visual complaints.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"What presentation raises suspicion of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis?","options":["Chronic wheeze after exercise","Persistent or double-worsening symptoms after an upper respiratory illness","An isolated headache with no nasal symptoms","One day of mild clear rhinorrhoea"],"answer":"B","explain":"Persistent or double-worsening symptoms after an upper respiratory illness. Persistent symptoms or double-worsening after a viral illness can indicate bacterial rhinosinusitis."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Why does sinus ostial obstruction promote bacterial infection?","options":["Impaired drainage and mucociliary clearance allow secretion stasis","It causes immediate pulmonary arterial thrombosis","It increases alveolar surfactant","It abolishes all nasal blood supply"],"answer":"A","explain":"Impaired drainage and mucociliary clearance allow secretion stasis. Blocked drainage and impaired mucus clearance promote retained secretions and secondary infection."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which anatomical sinus most plausibly causes cheek tenderness?","options":["Sphenoid sinus only","Mastoid air cells","Frontal sinus only","Maxillary sinus"],"answer":"D","explain":"Maxillary sinus. Maxillary sinusitis can cause cheek pain/pressure and maxillary tenderness."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which new symptom is a red flag for urgent referral?","options":["Clear rhinorrhoea alone","Periorbital swelling or visual disturbance","Mild transient congestion","Slight fatigue with normal examination"],"answer":"B","explain":"Periorbital swelling or visual disturbance. Orbital symptoms can indicate spread of sinus infection and threaten vision."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What is an appropriate initial student-level response in an uncomplicated stable patient?","options":["Immediate unsupervised sinus surgery","Assessment for severe features and supervised symptomatic care with antibiotic decisions according to local guidance","Empiric long-term antifungal treatment for all","Routine CT for every mild cold"],"answer":"B","explain":"Assessment for severe features and supervised symptomatic care with antibiotic decisions according to local guidance. Clinical assessment and severity/duration guide symptom relief and whether antibiotics are needed."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — At review he develops painful eye movement, eyelid oedema and reduced visual acuity. Which complication must be excluded urgently?","options":["Simple untreated allergic rhinitis","Stable COPD","Orbital cellulitis","Bronchiolitis"],"answer":"C","explain":"Orbital cellulitis. Painful eye movements and visual change suggest orbital extension and demand emergency assessment."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the drainage structure whose blockage predisposes to bacterial sinusitis.","answer":"Sinus ostium","explain":"Sinus ostium. An obstructed ostium prevents normal sinus mucus drainage."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the sinus located within the cheekbone region.","answer":"Maxillary sinus","explain":"Maxillary sinus. Maxillary sinus pathology commonly produces cheek tenderness."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-11","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 24-year-old woman reports recurrent bouts of sneezing, clear rhinorrhoea, itchy eyes and nasal obstruction every spring. She has no fever and normal oxygen saturation. Examination shows pale swollen nasal mucosa and watery secretions.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which clinical feature most strongly suggests allergy rather than a common viral cold?","options":["Transient malaise with fever and thick secretions","Recurrent itchy sneezing and watery rhinorrhoea linked to pollen exposure","Night sweats and weight loss","Progressive focal chest consolidation"],"answer":"B","explain":"Recurrent itchy sneezing and watery rhinorrhoea linked to pollen exposure. Itching and episodic allergen-associated symptoms point to allergic rhinitis."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"What hypersensitivity mechanism is typically involved?","options":["IgE-mediated mast-cell activation","Immune complex deposition as primary mechanism","Autoantibody destruction of red cells","Delayed type IV granulomatous infection"],"answer":"A","explain":"IgE-mediated mast-cell activation. Allergic rhinitis is a classic type I IgE-mediated hypersensitivity reaction."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mediator contributes importantly to acute sneezing and itching?","options":["Histamine","Fibrin","Troponin","Aldosterone"],"answer":"A","explain":"Histamine. Histamine released from mast cells acts at H1 receptors to produce itching and secretions."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which treatment effectively reduces persistent nasal mucosal inflammation?","options":["Loop diuretic","Routine systemic antibiotic for every episode","Oral anticoagulant","Intranasal corticosteroid"],"answer":"D","explain":"Intranasal corticosteroid. Topical intranasal steroids are an effective controller for persistent allergic inflammation."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which comorbidity should be asked about because of shared atopic airway inflammation?","options":["Ischaemic stroke","Chronic nephritis alone","Asthma","Valvular stenosis"],"answer":"C","explain":"Asthma. Allergic rhinitis commonly coexists with asthma as part of the atopic airway spectrum."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Nasal smear shows eosinophils; blood count shows a mildly raised eosinophil count. Which conclusion is best?","options":["They rule out asthma","They confirm bacterial sinusitis","Findings are compatible with allergic inflammation but not diagnostic alone","They prove TB infection"],"answer":"C","explain":"Findings are compatible with allergic inflammation but not diagnostic alone. Eosinophilia may support an allergic phenotype but must be interpreted in context."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the granulocyte associated with the late allergic inflammatory response.","answer":"Eosinophil","explain":"Eosinophil. Eosinophils contribute to persistent type-2 airway inflammation."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the primary mast-cell mediator responsible for itching and sneezing.","answer":"Histamine","explain":"Histamine. H1 receptor activation causes typical allergic nasal symptoms."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-12","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 53-year-old woman who had a hip replacement ten days ago develops sudden pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness. Pulse 124/min, respiratory rate 29/min, BP 118/76 mmHg, SpO2 90% on air. Her left calf is swollen and tender.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which diagnosis must be excluded urgently?","options":["Pulmonary embolism","Acute viral tonsillitis","Allergic rhinitis","Stable emphysema only"],"answer":"A","explain":"Pulmonary embolism. Sudden dyspnoea and pleuritic pain after surgery with unilateral leg swelling suggests PE from DVT."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which component of Virchow triad is strongly promoted by prolonged postoperative immobility?","options":["Isolated pulmonary vasodilation","Venous stasis","Reduced blood viscosity","Increased erythrocyte haemolysis"],"answer":"B","explain":"Venous stasis. Venous stasis from immobility favours thrombus formation."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the usual source of clinically important pulmonary emboli?","options":["Atherosclerotic plaque in the carotid artery","Thrombus in deep veins of the legs/pelvis","Bronchial secretions","Mitral valve calcification in all cases"],"answer":"B","explain":"Thrombus in deep veins of the legs/pelvis. Most PEs arise from lower-extremity/pelvic deep venous thromboses."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Why does acute PE cause hypoxaemia?","options":["Excess bronchial mucus is the only mechanism","Ventilation continues in regions with reduced perfusion, causing V/Q mismatch","A primary fall in inspired oxygen fraction","Alveoli universally fill with pus"],"answer":"B","explain":"Ventilation continues in regions with reduced perfusion, causing V/Q mismatch. PE creates high V/Q regions and worsens overall V/Q matching, leading to hypoxaemia."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which diagnostic strategy is most defensible?","options":["Ignore the postoperative history","Use D-dimer alone to exclude PE in every high-risk patient","Order routine sputum culture as the sole investigation","Urgent risk assessment with clinician-directed imaging/testing based on clinical probability"],"answer":"D","explain":"Urgent risk assessment with clinician-directed imaging/testing based on clinical probability. Clinical probability directs testing; likely PE often requires prompt imaging rather than relying on D-dimer."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — CT pulmonary angiography shows a filling defect in a segmental pulmonary artery. What is the interpretation?","options":["Caseating granuloma is demonstrated","Pulmonary arterial thromboembolism is demonstrated","Normal pulmonary vasculature","Definite myocardial infarction"],"answer":"B","explain":"Pulmonary arterial thromboembolism is demonstrated. An intraluminal pulmonary-artery filling defect on CTPA supports PE."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the leg-vein condition that commonly precedes pulmonary embolism.","answer":"Deep vein thrombosis","explain":"Deep vein thrombosis. DVT can embolise through the right heart to the pulmonary arteries."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the triad of endothelial injury, abnormal flow and hypercoagulability.","answer":"Virchow triad","explain":"Virchow triad. Virchow triad summarises factors predisposing to thrombosis."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-13","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 62-year-old patient admitted with cancer develops sudden syncope and severe breathlessness. BP 72/40 mmHg, heart rate 136/min, SpO2 84% on room air. Neck veins are distended and the lungs are relatively clear on auscultation.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Which type of shock best explains the possible PE in this case?","options":["Anaphylactic shock without trigger","Isolated haemorrhagic shock","Obstructive shock","Purely neurogenic shock"],"answer":"C","explain":"Obstructive shock. A large PE can acutely obstruct pulmonary circulation and impair RV output and LV preload."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"What is the immediate effect of a large clot on the right ventricle?","options":["Immediate fall in pulmonary arterial resistance","Abrupt increase in RV afterload","Obligate reduction in RV chamber size","Guaranteed increase in LV stroke volume"],"answer":"B","explain":"Abrupt increase in RV afterload. Acute pulmonary artery obstruction markedly increases RV pressure load."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which factor increases venous thromboembolism risk here?","options":["Active malignancy","Low dietary salt","Normal fasting glucose","History of seasonal pollen allergy"],"answer":"A","explain":"Active malignancy. Malignancy is prothrombotic and increases VTE risk."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Why can breath sounds remain relatively clear despite severe PE?","options":["The pleural cavity is necessarily flooded","Severe PE always causes bilateral wheezing","All alveoli must be filled with pus","The primary problem is perfusion rather than alveolar consolidation"],"answer":"D","explain":"The primary problem is perfusion rather than alveolar consolidation. PE principally impairs pulmonary blood flow, so crackles may be absent."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"What must be prioritised in this unstable patient?","options":["Immediate senior resuscitation support for suspected high-risk PE","Discharge after symptom control","Delay until next ward round","Routine outpatient D-dimer before acting"],"answer":"A","explain":"Immediate senior resuscitation support for suspected high-risk PE. Shock and hypoxaemia in possible PE are life-threatening and need urgent emergency-led assessment."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Bedside echo shows a dilated right ventricle with septal flattening and a small left ventricle. Which haemodynamic process is suggested?","options":["Isolated severe left-ventricular hypertrophy","Acute right-heart pressure overload reducing LV filling","Normal RV systolic pressure","Pure isolated airway bronchospasm"],"answer":"B","explain":"Acute right-heart pressure overload reducing LV filling. RV pressure overload and septal shift impede LV filling in high-risk PE."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the ventricle immediately strained by an acute large PE.","answer":"Right ventricle","explain":"Right ventricle. Acute pulmonary vascular obstruction raises right-ventricular afterload."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the pulmonary artery imaging test abbreviated CTPA.","answer":"CT pulmonary angiography","explain":"CT pulmonary angiography. CTPA can visualise pulmonary arterial filling defects."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"},{"id":"uploaded-p1-respiratory-14","paper":"Paper 1","module":"Respiratory","subject":"Respiratory","scenario":"A 58-year-old woman presents with gradually increasing breathlessness and right-sided pleuritic pain. Examination reveals reduced right basal chest expansion, stony dullness to percussion and diminished breath sounds. Ultrasound shows a moderate pleural effusion. She is stable enough for supervised fluid sampling.","questions":[{"number":1,"type":"SBA","q":"Where does pleural effusion fluid collect?","options":["Only within alveolar macrophages","Between the aortic intima and media","Between visceral and parietal pleura","Inside the main pulmonary artery"],"answer":"C","explain":"Between visceral and parietal pleura. The pleural space lies between the visceral and parietal pleural layers."},{"number":2,"type":"SBA","q":"Which mechanism commonly generates a transudative pleural effusion in heart failure?","options":["Increased hydrostatic pressure in the pulmonary/pleural circulation","Massive local inflammatory protein leakage alone","Direct invasion by tuberculosis in every case","Increased pleural capillary permeability from pneumonia only"],"answer":"A","explain":"Increased hydrostatic pressure in the pulmonary/pleural circulation. Elevated vascular hydrostatic pressure drives low-protein fluid into pleural space."},{"number":3,"type":"SBA","q":"Which finding makes a pleural exudate more likely?","options":["Normal pleural fluid volume","Higher fluid-to-serum protein or LDH ratios","Normal lung examination with no fluid","Low protein ratio with low LDH in all cases"],"answer":"B","explain":"Higher fluid-to-serum protein or LDH ratios. Local inflammation or malignancy increases protein/LDH in pleural fluid."},{"number":4,"type":"SBA","q":"Which pleural layer is innervated with pain fibres and responsible for pleuritic pain?","options":["Pulmonary arterioles","Visceral pleura","Parietal pleura","Alveolar surfactant layer"],"answer":"C","explain":"Parietal pleura. Somatic sensory innervation of parietal pleura causes sharp pleuritic pain."},{"number":5,"type":"SBA","q":"Which feature would prompt immediate escalation rather than routine diagnostic work-up?","options":["No new symptoms and normal oxygenation","Stable moderate effusion under evaluation","Worsening hypoxaemia and marked respiratory distress","Mild discomfort with normal observations"],"answer":"C","explain":"Worsening hypoxaemia and marked respiratory distress. Respiratory compromise takes priority over elective diagnostic procedures."},{"number":6,"type":"SBA","q":"DATA / RESULTS — Serum protein 66 g/L, pleural protein 36 g/L (ratio 0.545); pleural/serum LDH ratio 0.48. How is the fluid classified by Light criteria?","options":["Transudate because both ratios must be elevated","Empyema definitively","Exudate because one protein criterion is positive","Cannot use Light criteria without culture"],"answer":"C","explain":"Exudate because one protein criterion is positive. Light criteria require ANY one positive criterion; 36/66 = 0.545 > 0.5."},{"number":7,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the clinical percussion note expected over a moderate pleural effusion.","answer":"Stony dullness","explain":"Stony dullness. Pleural fluid produces classically stony-dull percussion."},{"number":8,"type":"VSAQ","q":"VSAQ — Name the enzyme assessed along with protein in Light criteria (abbreviation accepted).","answer":"Lactate dehydrogenase","explain":"Lactate dehydrogenase. Pleural and serum LDH are compared as part of Light criteria."}],"source":"Uploaded GEMP1 Paper 1 practice bank (original study questions, not official Wits past-paper questions)"}];
